i\\\": {\\n              \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"],\\n              \\\"format\\\": \\\"uri\\\"\\n            }\\n          }\\n        },\\n        \\\"parser_adversarial_probe\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": \\\"object\\\",\\n          \\\"additionalProperties\\\": false,\\n          \\\"required\\\": [\\n            \\\"method\\\",\\n            \\\"passed\\\",\\n            \\\"failure_modes\\\"\\n          ],\\n          \\\"properties\\\": {\\n            \\\"method\\\": {\\n              \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"]\\n            },\\n            \\\"passed\\\": {\\n              \\\"type\\\": [\\\"boolean\\\", \\\"null\\\"]\\n            },\\n            \\\"failure_modes\\\": {\\n              \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n              \\\"items\\\": {\\n                \\\"type\\\": \\\"string\\\"\\n              }\\n            },\\n            \\\"artifact_uri\\\": {\\n              \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"],\\n              \\\"format\\\": \\\"uri\\\"\\n            }\\n          }\\n        },\\n        \\\"evidence\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": \\\"array\\\",\\n          \\\"items\\\": {\\n            \\\"$ref\\\": \\\"#/$defs/Evidence\\\"\\n          }\\n        }\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \\\"AuditSummary\\\": {\\n      \\\"type\\\": \\\"object\\\",\\n      \\\"additionalProperties\\\": false,\\n      \\\"properties\\\": {\\n        \\\"audit_status\\\": {\\n          \\\"enum\\\": [\\n            \\\"not_audited\\\",\\n            \\\"planned\\\",\\n            \\\"running\\\",\\n            \\\"complete\\\",\\n            \\\"disputed\\\"\\n          ]\\n        },\\n        \\\"baseline_report_id\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"]\\n        },\\n        \\\"controlled_report_id\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"]\\n        },\\n        \\\"survival_verdict\\\": {\\n          \\\"enum\\\": [\\n            \\\"survives\\\",\\n            \\\"shrinks\\\",\\n            \\\"disappears\\\",\\n            \\\"reverses\\\",\\n            \\\"not_audited\\\",\\n            \\\"descriptive_only\\\",\\n            null\\n          ]\\n        },\\n        \\\"controlled_delta\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"number\\\", \\\"null\\\"]\\n        },\\n        \\\"confidence_interval_95\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"array\\\", \\\"null\\\"],\\n          \\\"items\\\": {\\n            \\\"type\\\": \\\"number\\\"\\n          },\\n          \\\"minItems\\\": 2,\\n          \\\"maxItems\\\": 2\\n        },\\n        \\\"notes\\\": {\\n          \\\"type\\\": [\\\"string\\\", \\\"null\\\"]\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Example entry: DAPO\\n\\nThis DAPO entry deliberately does **not** invent missing paper-specific values. The attached draft identifies DAPO’s registry-level defining delta as “clip-higher + dynamic sampling,” but its audit tables still contain TODO cells, so unknown values are represented as explicit `unknown` / `not_reported` fields. fileciteturn1file4\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": \\\"gvr.entry.v0.1.0\\\",\\n  \\\"registry_id\\\": \\\"gvr:dapo\\\",\\n  \\\"variant\\\": {\\n    \\\"canonical_name\\\": \\\"DAPO\\\",\\n    \\\"family\\\": \\\"GRPO-family\\\",\\n    \\\"base_algorithm\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n    \\\"aliases\\\": [\\\"DAPO\\\"],\\n    \\\"parents\\\": [\\\"GRPO\\\"],\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\n      \\\"loss-clip\\\",\\n      \\\"clip-higher\\\",\\n      \\\"dynamic-sampling\\\",\\n      \\\"group-relative-rl\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \\\"defining_delta\\\": {\\n    \\\"base_algorithm\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n    \\\"summary\\\": \\\"DAPO is registered as a GRPO-family variant whose defining delta is clip-higher plus dynamic sampling.\\\",\\n    \\\"delta_types\\\": [\\n      \\\"loss.clip.upper_asymmetry\\\",\\n      \\\"rollout.dynamic_sampling\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"hooks\\\": [\\n      {\\n        \\\"hook_id\\\": \\\"clip_higher\\\",\\n        \\\"hook_type\\\": \\\"loss_clip_rule\\\",\\n        \\\"semantics\\\": \\\"Use an asymmetric upper clipping rule relative to the GRPO baseline. Exact bound values must be supplied by the source report or audit implementation.\\\",\\n        \\\"target_min_report_rl_paths\\\": [\\n          \\\"/loss_form/clip/asymmetric\\\",\\n          \\\"/loss_form/clip/upper\\\",\\n          \\\"/loss_form/clip/clip_higher\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n          {\\n            \\\"name\\\": \\\"clip_upper\\\",\\n            \\\"status\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n            \\\"value\\\": null,\\n            \\\"unit\\\": null\\n          },\\n          {\\n            \\\"name\\\": \\\"clip_lower\\\",\\n            \\\"status\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n            \\\"value\\\": null,\\n            \\\"unit\\\": null\\n          }\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"hook_id\\\": \\\"dynamic_sampling\\\",\\n        \\\"hook_type\\\": \\\"rollout_selection_rule\\\",\\n        \\\"semantics\\\": \\\"Apply a dynamic rollout or sample-selection rule. Exact prompt/completion filtering, regeneration, or eligibility rule must be supplied by the source report or audit implementation.\\\",\\n        \\\"target_min_report_rl_paths\\\": [\\n          \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/dynamic_sampling\\\",\\n          \\\"/usable_signal_trajectory\\\",\\n          \\\"/group_size_schedule\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n          {\\n            \\\"name\\\": \\\"selection_rule\\\",\\n            \\\"status\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n            \\\"value\\\": null,\\n            \\\"unit\\\": null\\n          },\\n          {\\n            \\\"name\\\": \\\"resampling_budget\\\",\\n            \\\"status\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n            \\\"value\\\": null,\\n            \\\"unit\\\": \\\"rollouts\\\"\\n          }\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \\\"target_invariance\\\": {\\n      \\\"rule\\\": \\\"all_min_report_rl_except_delta_paths\\\",\\n      \\\"non_delta_paths_expected_constant\\\": [\\n        \\\"/reference_policy_kl\\\",\\n        \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/rollout_engine\\\",\\n        \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/backend_kind\\\",\\n        \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/sampler_precision\\\",\\n        \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/trainer_precision\\\",\\n        \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/tokenizer\\\",\\n        \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/chat_template\\\",\\n        \\\"/heldout_evaluation\\\",\\n        \\\"/decontamination_probe\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n    \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n      {\\n        \\\"kind\\\": \\\"paper\\\",\\n        \\\"status\\\": \\\"reported\\\",\\n        \\\"citation_key\\\": \\\"min_report_rl_draft\\\",\\n        \\\"uri\\\": null,\\n        \\\"locator\\\": \\\"Table 2: DAPO row\\\",\\n        \\\"sha256\\\": null,\\n        \\\"note\\\": \\\"The attached Min-Report-RL draft lists DAPO as 'clip-higher + dyn. sampling'.\\\"\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \\\"reports\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"report_id\\\": \\\"dapo-2025-as-published-stub\\\",\\n      \\\"report_type\\\": \\\"published\\\",\\n      \\\"lifecycle_status\\\": \\\"draft_stub\\\",\\n      \\\"source\\\": {\\n        \\\"source_type\\\": \\\"paper\\\",\\n        \\\"status\\\": \\\"partial\\\",\\n        \\\"title\\\": \\\"DAPO paper\\\",\\n        \\\"citation_key\\\": \\\"dapo2025\\\",\\n        \\\"uri\\\": null,\\n        \\\"version\\\": null,\\n        \\\"accessed_at\\\": null\\n      },\\n      \\\"implementation\\\": {\\n        \\\"trainer\\\": null,\\n        \\\"repo_uri\\\": null,\\n        \\\"commit\\\": null,\\n        \\\"config_uri\\\": null,\\n        \\\"artifact_bundle_uri\\\": null\\n      },\\n      \\\"min_report_rl\\\": {\\n        \\\"loss_form\\\": {\\n          \\\"status\\\": \\\"partial\\\",\\n          \\\"ppo_ratio\\\": null,\\n          \\\"ratio_granularity\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"clip\\\": {\\n            \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n            \\\"lower\\\": null,\\n            \\\"upper\\\": null,\\n            \\\"asymmetric\\\": true,\\n            \\\"clip_higher\\\": true,\\n            \\\"schedule\\\": null\\n          },\\n          \\\"token_mask\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"advantage_normalization\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"length_normalization\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"loss_equation_uri\\\": null,\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n            {\\n              \\\"kind\\\": \\\"paper\\\",\\n              \\\"status\\\": \\\"partial\\\",\\n              \\\"citation_key\\\": \\\"min_report_rl_draft\\\",\\n              \\\"uri\\\": null,\\n              \\\"locator\\\": \\\"Table 2 and Min-Report-RL item 1\\\",\\n              \\\"sha256\\\": null,\\n              \\\"note\\\": \\\"Only the clip-higher delta is available from the attached draft; other loss details remain unknown in this registry stub.\\\"\\n            }\\n          ]\\n        },\\n        \\\"reference_policy_kl\\\": {\\n          \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_reported\\\",\\n          \\\"reference_policy_retained\\\": null,\\n          \\\"reference_checkpoint\\\": null,\\n          \\\"kl_placement\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"kl_estimator\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"kl_coefficient\\\": null,\\n          \\\"kl_schedule\\\": null,\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n            {\\n              \\\"kind\\\": \\\"audit_note\\\",\\n              \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_reported\\\",\\n              \\\"citation_key\\\": \\\"min_report_rl_draft\\\",\\n              \\\"uri\\\": null,\\n              \\\"locator\\\": \\\"Registry stub\\\",\\n              \\\"sha256\\\": null,\\n              \\\"note\\\": \\\"Not specified in the attached draft's DAPO row.\\\"\\n            }\\n          ]\\n        },\\n        \\\"sampler_backend_precision\\\": {\\n          \\\"status\\\": \\\"partial\\\",\\n          \\\"rollout_engine\\\": null,\\n          \\\"backend_kind\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"decoding\\\": {\\n            \\\"temperature\\\": null,\\n            \\\"top_p\\\": null,\\n            \\\"top_k\\\": null,\\n            \\\"max_tokens\\\": null,\\n            \\\"seeded\\\": null\\n          },\\n          \\\"sampler_precision\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"trainer_precision\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"tokenizer\\\": {\\n            \\\"name_or_path\\\": null,\\n            \\\"revision\\\": null,\\n            \\\"shared_with_trainer\\\": null\\n          },\\n          \\\"chat_template\\\": {\\n            \\\"name\\\": null,\\n            \\\"sha256\\\": null\\n          },\\n          \\\"dynamic_sampling\\\": {\\n            \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n            \\\"rule\\\": null,\\n            \\\"rule_status\\\": \\\"unknown\\\"\\n          },\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n            {\\n              \\\"kind\\\": \\\"paper\\\",\\n              \\\"status\\\": \\\"partial\\\",\\n              \\\"citation_key\\\": \\\"min_report_rl_draft\\\",\\n              \\\"uri\\\": null,\\n              \\\"locator\\\": \\\"Table 2: DAPO row\\\",\\n              \\\"sha256\\\": null,\\n              \\\"note\\\": \\\"Dynamic sampling is listed as part of the DAPO defining delta; rollout engine and precision remain unknown here.\\\"\\n            }\\n          ]\\n        },\\n        \\\"usable_signal_trajectory\\\": {\\n          \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_reported\\\",\\n          \\\"metric_kind\\\": \\\"not_reported\\\",\\n          \\\"logged_per_step\\\": null,\\n          \\\"trajectory_uri\\\": null,\\n          \\\"trajectory_inline\\\": null,\\n          \\\"summary\\\": {\\n            \\\"mean_zvf_step_25\\\": null,\\n            \\\"mean_gu_step_25\\\": null,\\n            \\\"collapse_rate_seeds\\\": null,\\n            \\\"time_to_collapse_median_steps\\\": null,\\n            \\\"collapse_rule\\\": null\\n          },\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n            {\\n              \\\"kind\\\": \\\"audit_note\\\",\\n              \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_reported\\\",\\n              \\\"citation_key\\\": \\\"min_report_rl_draft\\\",\\n              \\\"uri\\\": null,\\n              \\\"locator\\\": \\\"Table 3 TODO cells\\\",\\n              \\\"sha256\\\": null,\\n              \\\"note\\\": \\\"The attached draft's telemetry table leaves DAPO ZVF/GU cells to be filled from the audit corpus.\\\"\\n            }\\n          ]\\n        },\\n        \\\"group_size_schedule\\\": {\\n          \\\"status\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"kind\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"initial_g\\\": null,\\n          \\\"final_g\\\": null,\\n          \\\"per_step_uri\\\": null,\\n          \\\"per_step_inline\\\": null,\\n          \\\"adaptive_rule\\\": null,\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n            {\\n              \\\"kind\\\": \\\"audit_note\\\",\\n              \\\"status\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n              \\\"citation_key\\\": \\\"min_report_rl_draft\\\",\\n              \\\"uri\\\": null,\\n              \\\"locator\\\": \\\"Table 3 TODO cells\\\",\\n              \\\"sha256\\\": null,\\n              \\\"note\\\": \\\"DAPO's dynamic sampling is known, but this stub does not infer a group-size schedule.\\\"\\n            }\\n          ]\\n        },\\n        \\\"heldout_evaluation\\\": {\\n          \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_reported\\\",\\n          \\\"reward_environment_disjoint\\\": null,\\n          \\\"dataset\\\": null,\\n          \\\"split\\\": null,\\n          \\\"n\\\": null,\\n          \\\"prompt_hash_manifest_uri\\\": null,\\n          \\\"evaluator\\\": null,\\n          \\\"metrics\\\": [],\\n          \\\"checkpoint_selection\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n            {\\n              \\\"kind\\\": \\\"audit_note\\\",\\n              \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_reported\\\",\\n              \\\"citation_key\\\": \\\"min_report_rl_draft\\\",\\n              \\\"uri\\\": null,\\n              \\\"locator\\\": \\\"Table 2 TODO cells\\\",\\n              \\\"sha256\\\": null,\\n              \\\"note\\\": \\\"Controlled held-out results are placeholders in the attached draft.\\\"\\n            }\\n          ]\\n        },\\n        \\\"decontamination_probe\\\": {\\n          \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_reported\\\",\\n          \\\"train_test_overlap_probe\\\": {\\n            \\\"method\\\": null,\\n            \\\"passed\\\": null,\\n            \\\"max_overlap\\\": null,\\n            \\\"artifact_uri\\\": null\\n          },\\n          \\\"parser_adversarial_probe\\\": {\\n            \\\"method\\\": null,\\n            \\\"passed\\\": null,\\n            \\\"failure_modes\\\": [],\\n            \\\"artifact_uri\\\": null\\n          },\\n          \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n            {\\n              \\\"kind\\\": \\\"audit_note\\\",\\n              \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_reported\\\",\\n              \\\"citation_key\\\": \\\"min_report_rl_draft\\\",\\n              \\\"uri\\\": null,\\n              \\\"locator\\\": \\\"Registry stub\\\",\\n              \\\"sha256\\\": null,\\n              \\\"note\\\": \\\"No DAPO-specific decontamination or parser-probe results are provided in the attached draft.\\\"\\n            }\\n          ]\\n        }\\n      },\\n      \\\"results\\\": {\\n        \\\"published_delta\\\": null,\\n        \\\"controlled_last10\\\": null,\\n        \\\"controlled_heldout\\\": null,\\n        \\\"controlled_delta\\\": null,\\n        \\\"confidence_interval_95\\\": null,\\n        \\\"survival_verdict\\\": \\\"not_audited\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"completeness\\\": {\\n        \\\"required_items_present\\\": 7,\\n        \\\"required_items_reported\\\": 1,\\n        \\\"missing_paths\\\": [\\n          \\\"/loss_form/ppo_ratio\\\",\\n          \\\"/loss_form/ratio_granularity\\\",\\n          \\\"/loss_form/token_mask\\\",\\n          \\\"/loss_form/advantage_normalization\\\",\\n          \\\"/reference_policy_kl\\\",\\n          \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/rollout_engine\\\",\\n          \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/sampler_precision\\\",\\n          \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/trainer_precision\\\",\\n          \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/tokenizer\\\",\\n          \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/chat_template\\\",\\n          \\\"/usable_signal_trajectory\\\",\\n          \\\"/group_size_schedule\\\",\\n          \\\"/heldout_evaluation\\\",\\n          \\\"/decontamination_probe\\\"\\n        ],\\n        \\\"completeness_score\\\": 0.14\\n      }\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"audit_summary\\\": {\\n    \\\"audit_status\\\": \\\"planned\\\",\\n    \\\"baseline_report_id\\\": null,\\n    \\\"controlled_report_id\\\": null,\\n    \\\"survival_verdict\\\": \\\"not_audited\\\",\\n    \\\"controlled_delta\\\": null,\\n    \\\"confidence_interval_95\\\": null,\\n    \\\"notes\\\": \\\"The attached Min-Report-RL paper proposes a controlled single-stack audit; numeric DAPO results are not yet filled.\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"governance\\\": {\\n    \\\"entry_status\\\": \\\"draft_stub\\\",\\n    \\\"created_at\\\": \\\"2026-06-21T00:00:00Z\\\",\\n    \\\"updated_at\\\": \\\"2026-06-21T00:00:00Z\\\",\\n    \\\"maintainers\\\": [\\\"grpo-registry-maintainers\\\"],\\n    \\\"change_log_uri\\\": null\\n  },\\n  \\\"extensions\\\": {}\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Query API\\n\\n### Core objects\\n\\nThe registry exposes three main resources:\\n\\n```text\\nVariantEntry\\n  └── reports[]\\n        └── min_report_rl\\n              ├── loss_form\\n              ├── reference_policy_kl\\n              ├── sampler_backend_precision\\n              ├── usable_signal_trajectory\\n              ├── group_size_schedule\\n              ├── heldout_evaluation\\n              └── decontamination_probe\\n```\\n\\nThis follows the paper’s audit framing: for each variant, store both “as published” and “controlled stack” readings when available, and judge survival by the controlled delta against a shared GRPO baseline. fileciteturn1file4\\n\\n---\\n\\n### REST endpoints\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/variants\\n```\\n\\nList variants.\\n\\nSupported query parameters:\\n\\n```text\\nq                         free-text search\\nfamily                    e.g. GRPO-family\\nbase_algorithm            e.g. GRPO\\ndelta_type                e.g. loss.clip.upper_asymmetry\\ntag                       e.g. dynamic-sampling\\nentry_status              draft_stub | verified | disputed | superseded | retracted\\naudit_status              not_audited | planned | running | complete | disputed\\nsurvival_verdict          survives | shrinks | disappears | reverses | not_audited\\nmin_completeness          float from 0 to 1\\ninclude                   reports,audit_summary,defining_delta\\nlimit                     page size\\ncursor                    pagination cursor\\n```\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/variants?base_algorithm=GRPO&delta_type=loss.clip.upper_asymmetry&include=defining_delta,audit_summary\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/variants/{registry_id}\\n```\\n\\nFetch one full registry entry.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/variants/gvr:dapo?include=reports\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/reports\\n```\\n\\nSearch report-level stack records.\\n\\nSupported query parameters:\\n\\n```text\\nvariant                   registry id, e.g. gvr:dapo\\nreport_type               published | controlled_audit | third_party_replication\\ntrainer                   TRL | verl | OpenRLHF | custom\\nrollout_engine            vLLM | SGLang | managed_api | trainer_generate\\nbackend_kind              open | managed | closed | mixed\\ntoken_mask                completion_only | whole_sequence | custom | unknown\\nkl_placement              loss | reward | both | absent | unknown\\nsampler_precision         fp32 | bf16 | fp16 | mixed | unknown\\ntrainer_precision         fp32 | bf16 | fp16 | mixed | unknown\\ngroup_schedule_kind       fixed | adaptive | curriculum | unknown\\nzvf_gu_available          true | false\\nheldout_disjoint          true | false\\ndecontamination_passed    true | false\\nmissing                   JSON Pointer path, e.g. /reference_policy_kl\\n```\\n\\nExample: find DAPO-like reports that actually logged ZVF/GU.\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/reports?delta_type=rollout.dynamic_sampling&zvf_gu_available=true\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/reports/{report_id}/min-report-rl\\n```\\n\\nReturn only the seven-field Min-Report-RL block.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/reports/dapo-2025-as-published-stub/min-report-rl\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/reports/{report_id}/telemetry/usable-signal\\n```\\n\\nStream per-step telemetry when available.\\n\\nQuery parameters:\\n\\n```text\\nmetric                    zvf_gu | reward_variance | gradient_norm_variance\\nfrom_step                 integer\\nto_step                   integer\\nformat                    jsonl | csv | parquet\\ncursor                    pagination cursor\\n```\\n\\nExample JSONL row:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"report_id\\\": \\\"dapo-controlled-audit-001\\\",\\n  \\\"step\\\": 25,\\n  \\\"zvf\\\": 0.62,\\n  \\\"gu\\\": 0.38,\\n  \\\"mean_reward\\\": 0.41,\\n  \\\"n_prompts\\\": 512\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Comparability API\\n\\nThe most important endpoint is not simple search; it is **stack comparability**.\\n\\n```http\\nPOST /v1/compare/stack-equivalence\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n```\\n\\nRequest:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"baseline_report_id\\\": \\\"grpo-baseline-controlled-001\\\",\\n  \\\"candidate_report_id\\\": \\\"dapo-controlled-audit-001\\\",\\n  \\\"rule\\\": \\\"all_min_report_rl_except_delta_paths\\\",\\n  \\\"delta_paths\\\": [\\n    \\\"/loss_form/clip/asymmetric\\\",\\n    \\\"/loss_form/clip/upper\\\",\\n    \\\"/loss_form/clip/clip_higher\\\",\\n    \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/dynamic_sampling\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"require_evidence\\\": true\\n}\\n```\\n\\nResponse:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"stack_equivalent\\\": false,\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"not_comparable\\\",\\n  \\\"matched_paths\\\": [\\n    \\\"/reference_policy_kl/kl_placement\\\",\\n    \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/rollout_engine\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"differing_paths\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"/sampler_backend_precision/trainer_precision\\\",\\n      \\\"baseline_value\\\": \\\"bf16\\\",\\n      \\\"candidate_value\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"blocking\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"missing_paths\\\": [\\n    \\\"/decontamination_probe\\\",\\n    \\\"/heldout_evaluation/prompt_hash_manifest_uri\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Candidate report is missing fields needed to assert that only the DAPO delta changed.\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Structured search API\\n\\nFor richer public queries:\\n\\n```http\\nPOST /v1/search\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n```\\n\\nExample: “Find GRPO-family variants whose defining delta touches clipping and whose controlled audit survived.”\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"where\\\": {\\n    \\\"variant.base_algorithm\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n    \\\"defining_delta.delta_types\\\": {\\n      \\\"contains\\\": \\\"loss.clip.upper_asymmetry\\\"\\n    },\\n    \\\"reports\\\": {\\n      \\\"some\\\": {\\n        \\\"report_type\\\": \\\"controlled_audit\\\",\\n        \\\"results.survival_verdict\\\": {\\n          \\\"in\\\": [\\\"survives\\\", \\\"shrinks\\\"]\\n        },\\n        \\\"completeness.completeness_score\\\": {\\n          \\\"gte\\\": 0.95\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"select\\\": [\\n    \\\"registry_id\\\",\\n    \\\"variant.canonical_name\\\",\\n    \\\"defining_delta.summary\\\",\\n    \\\"audit_summary.survival_verdict\\\",\\n    \\\"audit_summary.controlled_delta\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"order_by\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"audit_summary.controlled_delta\\\",\\n      \\\"direction\\\": \\\"desc\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"limit\\\": 50\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Missing-report query\\n\\nThis supports the paper’s adoption goal: make omissions visible and auditable.\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/reports?missing=/usable_signal_trajectory&base_algorithm=GRPO\\n```\\n\\nResponse sketch:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"data\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"report_id\\\": \\\"dapo-2025-as-published-stub\\\",\\n      \\\"variant\\\": \\\"DAPO\\\",\\n      \\\"missing_paths\\\": [\\n        \\\"/usable_signal_trajectory\\\",\\n        \\\"/heldout_evaluation\\\",\\n        \\\"/decontamination_probe\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"completeness_score\\\": 0.14\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"next_cursor\\\": null\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Canonical registry queries\\n\\nThese are the queries I’d expect reviewers and audit authors to use most:\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/variants?base_algorithm=GRPO&include=audit_summary\\n```\\n\\nShow all GRPO-family variants and their survival status.\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/reports?variant=gvr:dapo&report_type=published\\n```\\n\\nShow DAPO as published.\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/reports?variant=gvr:dapo&report_type=controlled_audit\\n```\\n\\nShow DAPO in controlled single-stack audits.\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/reports?missing=/reference_policy_kl\\n```\\n\\nFind papers that do not say where KL lives.\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/reports?token_mask=whole_sequence&heldout_disjoint=false\\n```\\n\\nFind potentially confounded reports using whole-sequence loss and no disjoint held-out split.\\n\\n```http\\nPOST /v1/compare/stack-equivalence\\n```\\n\\nAsk whether a DAPO-vs-GRPO comparison really differs only by DAPO’s declared delta.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Registry invariants\\n\\nThe public registry should enforce these invariants:\\n\\n1. **No silent omissions.** All seven Min-Report-RL fields must be present in every `report`.\\n2. **Unknown is a value.** `unknown` and `not_reported` are valid but queryable.\\n3. **No result without stack.** A `controlled_delta` may be present only if the corresponding `controlled_audit` report contains a full `min_report_rl` block.\\n4. **No comparability by label.** Two reports are comparable only if `/v1/compare/stack-equivalence` passes after ignoring the declared delta paths.\\n5. **Telemetry is externalizable.** Full per-step ZVF/GU can live in a linked artifact, but the report must state whether it exists and provide summaries.\\n6. **Published and controlled readings are separate.** A variant can have one as-published stack and multiple controlled-audit stacks.\\n7. **Delta paths are machine-readable.** Every defining delta must point to the Min-Report-RL paths it changes, so audits can mechanically check “all else equal.”\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a379b16-0aec-83e8-8ce1-3bb69849d800\",\"title\":\"CLI Tool for GRPO Diff\",\"current_node\":\"5800ece7-89c6-43b7-b1b1-de814f25874d\",\"n_nodes\":27,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"ct\":\"text\",\"len\":40677,\"t\":\"Read the attached paper. Invent a CLI tool that takes two GRPO run manifests and reports which stack levers differ and whether each difference is large enough to flip a comparison. Define the diff taxonomy and output format.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-621\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | \\n512 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n513 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n514 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n515 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n516 | appendix.\\n517 | \\n518 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n519 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n520 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n521 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n522 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n523 | \\n524 | % =============================================================================\\n525 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n526 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n527 | % =============================================================================\\n528 | \\n529 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n530 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n531 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n532 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n533 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n534 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n535 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n536 | \\n537 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n538 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n539 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n540 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n541 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n542 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n543 | justify it the same way.}\\n544 | \\n545 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n546 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n547 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n548 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n549 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n550 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n551 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n552 | \\n553 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n554 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n555 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n556 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n557 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n558 | algorithm.\\n559 | \\n560 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n561 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n562 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n563 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n564 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n565 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n566 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n567 | reported.\\n568 | \\n569 | % =============================================================================\\n570 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n571 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n572 | % =============================================================================\\n573 | \\n574 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n575 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n576 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n577 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n578 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n579 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n580 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n581 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n582 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n583 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n584 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n585 | can actually trust.\\n586 | \\n587 | % =============================================================================\\n588 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n589 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n590 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n591 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n592 | %\\n593 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n594 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n595 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n596 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n597 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n598 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n599 | % =============================================================================\\n600 | \\\\section*{References}\\n601 | \\\\small\\n602 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n603 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n604 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n605 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n606 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n607 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n608 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n609 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n610 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n611 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n612 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n613 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n614 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n615 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n616 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n617 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n618 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n619 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n620 | \\n621 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"code\",\"len\":491,\"t\":\"{\\\"queries\\\":[\\\"+(Min-Report-RL) seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist flip comparison loss form KL sampler backend precision Zvf Gu group-size held-out decontamination --QDF=1\\\",\\\"+(stack-conditioning) GRPO run manifest differs flip levers backend sampler KL reward parser token mask group-size --QDF=1\\\",\\\"+(controlled audit) survival verdict survives shrinks disappears reverses published controlled delta confidence interval --QDF=1\\\"],\\\"source_filter\\\":[\\\"files_uploaded_in_conversation\\\"]}\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"text\",\"len\":17518,\"t\":\"## Tool: `grpo-stackdiff`\\n\\n`grpo-stackdiff` is a reviewer/auditor CLI for answering one question:\\n\\n> Are these two “GRPO” runs a clean method comparison, or are they two different stacks wearing the same label?\\n\\nIt is built around the paper’s premise that an algorithm label is not a complete treatment description: runs can differ in loss form, KL/reference handling, sampler/backend/precision, usable-signal telemetry, group schedule, held-out evaluation, and decontamination/parser evidence, each of which the paper treats as a flip-capable lever. fileciteturn0file0 fileciteturn1file4 fileciteturn1file5\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1. CLI shape\\n\\n```bash\\ngrpo-stackdiff diff RUN_A.yaml RUN_B.yaml \\\\\\n  --metric results.heldout.exact_match \\\\\\n  --claim \\\"A > B\\\" \\\\\\n  --allow-delta loss.clip.upper \\\\\\n  --allow-delta sampling.dynamic_rule \\\\\\n  --calibration zvf-stack-effects.yaml \\\\\\n  --format text,json \\\\\\n  --out report.json\\n```\\n\\nSubcommands:\\n\\n```bash\\ngrpo-stackdiff validate RUN.yaml\\ngrpo-stackdiff diff RUN_A.yaml RUN_B.yaml\\ngrpo-stackdiff explain MRR-3.rollout.backend\\ngrpo-stackdiff schema --format jsonschema\\ngrpo-stackdiff calibrate matched_ablation_runs/*.yaml --out effects.yaml\\n```\\n\\nThe key design choice: the tool distinguishes **intended method deltas** from **uncontrolled stack deltas**. For example, comparing DAPO to GRPO may intentionally allow `loss.clip.upper` or `sampling.dynamic_rule`, but a hidden change in tokenizer, reward parser, held-out split, KL placement, or sampler precision is reported as a stack confound. That mirrors the paper’s controlled-audit rule: hold everything identical except the variant’s defining hook. fileciteturn1file7\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 2. Required run manifest shape\\n\\nThe CLI consumes JSON or YAML. The manifest is essentially the paper’s “cost is a JSON block” made concrete.\\n\\n```yaml\\nschema_version: min-report-rl.manifest/v0.1\\n\\nrun:\\n  id: qwen3_grpo_trl_seed42\\n  algorithm_label: GRPO\\n  framework: trl\\n  framework_version: 0.XX\\n  trainer_commit: 7d9c...\\n  seed_set: [42, 43, 44]\\n\\nmodel:\\n  base_checkpoint: Qwen/Qwen3-8B\\n  base_checkpoint_sha: sha256:...\\n  tokenizer_id: Qwen/Qwen3-8B\\n  tokenizer_sha: sha256:...\\n  chat_template_sha: sha256:...\\n\\nloss:\\n  importance_ratio: ppo_token_ratio        # none | ppo_token_ratio | sequence_ratio\\n  clipping:\\n    enabled: true\\n    lower: 0.8\\n    upper: 1.2\\n    asymmetric_clip_higher: null\\n  token_mask: completion_only              # completion_only | whole_sequence\\n  advantage_norm: per_group                # per_group | per_batch | running\\n  length_normalization: token_mean\\n  variant_hooks: []\\n\\nreference_kl:\\n  frozen_reference: true\\n  reference_checkpoint_sha: sha256:...\\n  placement: loss                          # loss | reward | absent\\n  coefficient: 0.02\\n  schedule: constant\\n  estimator: forward_token                 # forward_token | reverse_token | seq_kl\\n\\nrollout:\\n  backend: vllm\\n  backend_version: 0.XX\\n  decoding:\\n    temperature: 0.7\\n    top_p: 0.95\\n    max_tokens: 512\\n  sampler_logit_precision: bf16\\n  trainer_logit_precision: bf16\\n  tokenizer_shared_with_trainer: true\\n  chat_template_shared_with_trainer: true\\n\\ngroup:\\n  schedule:\\n    type: fixed\\n    values: [{start_step: 0, G: 8}]\\n  adaptive_rule: null\\n\\nreward:\\n  environment_id: gsm8k_train_500\\n  parser_name: gsm8k_boxed_answer\\n  parser_sha: sha256:...\\n  parser_format_only_probe:\\n    adversarial_pass_rate: 0.00\\n    n: 200\\n\\nsignal_telemetry:\\n  zvf:\\n    path: traces/zvf.jsonl\\n    first_5_mean: 0.21\\n    step_25_mean: 0.34\\n  gu:\\n    path: traces/gu.jsonl\\n    first_5_mean: 0.79\\n    step_25_mean: 0.66\\n  collapse_rule:\\n    zvf_ge_80_reward_le_5_first_5: false\\n\\ntraining:\\n  optimizer: adamw\\n  optimizer_config_sha: sha256:...\\n  lora:\\n    enabled: true\\n    rank: 16\\n    alpha: 32\\n    target_modules: [q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj]\\n  precision: bf16\\n  steps: 30\\n  learning_rate: 1.0e-5\\n\\neval:\\n  heldout:\\n    dataset_id: gsm8k_test_500\\n    disjoint_from_reward_env: true\\n    overlap_probe_sha: sha256:...\\n    n: 500\\n    metric: exact_match\\n    value: 0.833\\n    ci95: [0.801, 0.862]\\n  harness_name: lm-eval-custom\\n  harness_sha: sha256:...\\n  checkpoint_selection: final_step          # final_step | best_train_reward | best_heldout\\n\\nresults:\\n  last10_train_reward: 0.844\\n  primary_metric_path: eval.heldout.value\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3. Diff taxonomy\\n\\nThe tool uses a three-layer taxonomy: **lever**, **diff kind**, and **comparison role**.\\n\\n## 3.1 Lever taxonomy\\n\\n| Code | Lever | Examples | Default risk |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| `MRR-1.loss` | Loss form | ratio on/off, token vs sequence ratio, clip bounds, token mask, advantage normalization, length normalization | Critical |\\n| `MRR-2.ref_kl` | Reference policy and KL | frozen ref present/absent, KL in loss vs reward, coefficient, schedule, estimator | Critical |\\n| `MRR-3.rollout` | Sampler/backend/precision | vLLM vs managed API, decoding params, bf16/fp32 logits, tokenizer/template sharing | Critical |\\n| `MRR-4.signal` | Usable-signal telemetry | ZVF/Gu traces, collapse status, early mixed-group availability | Critical |\\n| `MRR-5.group` | Group-size schedule | fixed `G`, adaptive rule, per-step schedule, compute spent per prompt | High |\\n| `MRR-6.eval` | Held-out evaluation | disjointness, sample size, CI, harness, checkpoint selection | Critical |\\n| `MRR-7.decontam` | Decontamination and parser probes | train/test overlap, parser adversarial behavior, format-only shortcuts | High/Critical |\\n| `EXT-1.identity` | Model/tokenizer/template identity | checkpoint hash, tokenizer hash, chat template hash | Critical |\\n| `EXT-2.adapter_optimizer` | LoRA, optimizer, training precision | target modules, rank, optimizer defaults, bf16/fp16/fp32 trainer precision | High |\\n| `EXT-3.reward_env` | Reward environment and parser identity | reward dataset, parser code hash, reward service version | Critical |\\n| `EXT-4.selection` | Run/checkpoint selection | best training reward vs final step vs pre-registered checkpoint | Critical |\\n\\nThe first seven are the Min-Report-RL minimum stack. The `EXT-*` categories are included because the paper repeatedly names them as stack components that must be held fixed in a controlled audit: checkpoint/tokenizer, LoRA targets/rank, optimizer, reward parser, evaluator, and checkpoint selection. fileciteturn1file14 fileciteturn1file7\\n\\n## 3.2 Diff-kind taxonomy\\n\\n| Kind | Meaning | Example |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `VALUE_DIFF` | Categorical value differs | `token_mask: whole_sequence → completion_only` |\\n| `NUMERIC_SHIFT` | Numeric field differs | `temperature: 0.7 → 1.0` |\\n| `SCHEDULE_SHIFT` | Per-step schedule differs | `G=8 fixed → adaptive 4/8/16` |\\n| `ARTIFACT_HASH_DIFF` | Same human name, different artifact hash | same checkpoint label, different SHA |\\n| `CODE_PATH_DIFF` | Different implementation code | different reward parser commit |\\n| `TELEMETRY_DIST_DIFF` | Trace/distribution differs | ZVF first-5 mean `0.82 → 0.19` |\\n| `EVAL_INCOMPARABLE` | Metrics are not the same estimand | training reward vs held-out exact match |\\n| `EVIDENCE_MISSING` | Required field absent in one or both manifests | no KL placement reported |\\n| `SEMANTIC_EQUIV` | Surface diff canonicalizes to same meaning | `fp16` alias resolved to `float16` |\\n\\n## 3.3 Comparison-role taxonomy\\n\\n| Role | Meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| `INTENDED_METHOD_DELTA` | The difference is the claimed algorithmic hook, supplied by `--allow-delta` or the manifest. |\\n| `STACK_CONFOUND` | Difference is outside the intended method delta and can contaminate the comparison. |\\n| `MEASUREMENT_CONFOUND` | Difference changes how performance is measured, selected, or validated. |\\n| `REPORTING_GAP` | Field is missing, so the comparison cannot be audited. |\\n| `BENIGN_EQUIVALENT` | Difference is normalized away or demonstrably immaterial. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 4. “Large enough to flip” rule\\n\\n`grpo-stackdiff` does not pretend that two manifests alone identify causal effects. It reports flip materiality using explicit evidence.\\n\\nFor a comparison metric:\\n\\n```text\\ndelta_pp = 100 * (metric_A - metric_B)\\npoint_flip_threshold_pp = abs(delta_pp)\\n```\\n\\nA stack difference is marked **large enough to flip the point comparison** when:\\n\\n```text\\neffect_upper_bound_pp >= point_flip_threshold_pp\\n```\\n\\nThe effect bound can come from four sources, in order of preference:\\n\\n1. **Matched ablation calibration**: user-provided or corpus-derived effect sizes for a lever in a comparable model/task/backend setting.\\n2. **Analytic signal estimate**: used for ZVF/Gu and group-size changes, using the mixed-group probability logic from the paper.\\n3. **Paper-prior heuristic**: optional mode, disabled by default, that treats the paper’s documented flip mechanisms as broad priors.\\n4. **Unknown**: no numeric bound; the tool reports `UNKNOWN_BLOCKING` rather than inventing certainty.\\n\\nThe paper’s ZVF/Gu discussion is important here: usable group-relative signal depends on mixed-reward groups, and a run can appear to win simply because its stack produced more usable groups rather than because its algorithmic change is better. fileciteturn1file13\\n\\nVerdicts:\\n\\n| Verdict | `large_enough_to_flip` | Meaning |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| `FLIPS_POINT` | `true` | Calibrated or structural effect is at least as large as the observed point margin. |\\n| `ERASES_CLAIM` | `partial` | May not reverse the point estimate, but is large enough to erase a CI-based claim. |\\n| `NEAR_MARGIN` | `partial` | Effect upper bound is at least half the margin; needs controlled rerun. |\\n| `NO_EVIDENCE_OF_FLIP` | `false` | Calibrated upper bound is below the flip threshold. |\\n| `UNKNOWN_BLOCKING` | `null` | Required field missing or no calibrated bound for a mandatory flip lever. |\\n| `INCOMPARABLE` | `null` | Runs use different evaluation/reward environments or non-equivalent metrics. |\\n\\nFor structural changes such as different held-out splits, different reward parsers, absent KL disclosure, or training reward compared to held-out reward, the tool does not reduce the issue to a fake numeric estimate. It reports `INCOMPARABLE` or `UNKNOWN_BLOCKING`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5. Text output format\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n```text\\ngrpo-stackdiff 0.1.0\\ncomparison: dapo_run.yaml (A) vs grpo_baseline.yaml (B)\\nprimary metric: eval.heldout.exact_match\\nA: 84.6%   B: 82.1%   observed margin: +2.5 pp\\nclaim: A > B\\nallowed method deltas:\\n  - loss.clipping.upper\\n  - group.adaptive_rule\\n\\nOVERALL VERDICT: STACK-CONFOUNDED\\nReason: 3 uncontrolled stack differences are individually flip-capable or blocking.\\n\\n┌──────┬──────────────────────────┬──────────────┬────────────────────────────┬─────────────┬──────────────┬────────────────────┐\\n│ ID   │ Lever                    │ Role         │ Difference                 │ Effect bound│ Flip verdict │ Action             │\\n├──────┼──────────────────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────────────┼─────────────┼──────────────┼────────────────────┤\\n│ D001 │ MRR-1.loss.clip          │ INTENDED     │ upper: 1.20 → 1.28         │ unscored    │ method delta │ keep as claimed    │\\n│ D002 │ MRR-2.ref_kl.placement   │ CONFOUND     │ loss → reward              │ unknown     │ UNKNOWN_BLOCKING │ rerun same KL  │\\n│ D003 │ MRR-3.rollout.backend    │ CONFOUND     │ vllm → managed_api         │ 5–80 pp     │ FLIPS_POINT  │ same sampler       │\\n│ D004 │ MRR-4.signal.zvf_first5  │ CONFOUND     │ 0.82 → 0.21; collapse flip │ ≥10 pp      │ FLIPS_POINT  │ inspect signal     │\\n│ D005 │ MRR-6.eval.checkpoint    │ MEASUREMENT  │ final_step → best_train    │ structural  │ INCOMPARABLE │ pre-register ckpt  │\\n│ D006 │ MRR-7.decontam.probe     │ REPORT_GAP   │ missing in A               │ unknown     │ UNKNOWN_BLOCKING │ run probe      │\\n└──────┴──────────────────────────┴──────────────┴────────────────────────────┴─────────────┴──────────────┴────────────────────┘\\n\\nClean-method-comparison status:\\n  FAIL: uncontrolled differences remain outside the allowed method delta.\\n\\nMinimal rerun recipe:\\n  1. Use the same rollout backend, tokenizer, chat template, and logit precision.\\n  2. Put KL in the same location with the same reference checkpoint and beta schedule.\\n  3. Select checkpoints by the same pre-registered rule.\\n  4. Report ZVF/Gu traces for both arms.\\n  5. Run the same decontamination and parser probes.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 6. JSON output format\\n\\nMachine-readable output is the canonical format. The text report is a rendering of this.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema_version\\\": \\\"grpo-stackdiff.report/v0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"tool\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"grpo-stackdiff\\\",\\n    \\\"version\\\": \\\"0.1.0\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"inputs\\\": {\\n    \\\"left\\\": \\\"dapo_run.yaml\\\",\\n    \\\"right\\\": \\\"grpo_baseline.yaml\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"comparison\\\": {\\n    \\\"left_label\\\": \\\"A\\\",\\n    \\\"right_label\\\": \\\"B\\\",\\n    \\\"claim\\\": \\\"A > B\\\",\\n    \\\"primary_metric_path\\\": \\\"eval.heldout.exact_match\\\",\\n    \\\"left_value\\\": 0.846,\\n    \\\"right_value\\\": 0.821,\\n    \\\"delta_pp\\\": 2.5,\\n    \\\"point_flip_threshold_pp\\\": 2.5,\\n    \\\"metric_quality\\\": \\\"heldout_disjoint_with_ci\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"allowed_method_deltas\\\": [\\n    \\\"loss.clipping.upper\\\",\\n    \\\"group.adaptive_rule\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"overall\\\": {\\n    \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"STACK_CONFOUNDED\\\",\\n    \\\"clean_method_comparison\\\": false,\\n    \\\"diff_count\\\": 6,\\n    \\\"uncontrolled_diff_count\\\": 5,\\n    \\\"single_lever_flip_count\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"blocking_unknown_count\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"incomparable_count\\\": 1\\n  },\\n  \\\"diffs\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"D001\\\",\\n      \\\"lever\\\": {\\n        \\\"code\\\": \\\"MRR-1.loss\\\",\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"Loss form\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"paths\\\": [\\n        \\\"loss.clipping.upper\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"kind\\\": \\\"NUMERIC_SHIFT\\\",\\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"INTENDED_METHOD_DELTA\\\",\\n      \\\"left\\\": 1.28,\\n      \\\"right\\\": 1.2,\\n      \\\"normalized_delta\\\": {\\n        \\\"unit\\\": \\\"ratio\\\",\\n        \\\"value\\\": 0.08\\n      },\\n      \\\"materiality\\\": {\\n        \\\"risk\\\": \\\"unscored_intended_delta\\\",\\n        \\\"effect_interval_pp\\\": null,\\n        \\\"effect_source\\\": \\\"not_applicable\\\",\\n        \\\"large_enough_to_flip\\\": null,\\n        \\\"flip_verdict\\\": \\\"METHOD_DELTA\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Allowed by comparison intent; this is part of the claimed DAPO-style hook.\\\",\\n      \\\"recommended_action\\\": \\\"Keep, but label it as the intended algorithmic delta.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"D002\\\",\\n      \\\"lever\\\": {\\n        \\\"code\\\": \\\"MRR-2.ref_kl\\\",\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"Reference policy and KL handling\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"paths\\\": [\\n        \\\"reference_kl.placement\\\",\\n        \\\"reference_kl.coefficient\\\",\\n        \\\"reference_kl.estimator\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"kind\\\": \\\"VALUE_DIFF\\\",\\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"STACK_CONFOUND\\\",\\n      \\\"left\\\": {\\n        \\\"placement\\\": \\\"reward\\\",\\n        \\\"coefficient\\\": 0.02,\\n        \\\"estimator\\\": \\\"forward_token\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"right\\\": {\\n        \\\"placement\\\": \\\"loss\\\",\\n        \\\"coefficient\\\": 0.02,\\n        \\\"estimator\\\": \\\"forward_token\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"materiality\\\": {\\n        \\\"risk\\\": \\\"critical\\\",\\n        \\\"effect_interval_pp\\\": null,\\n        \\\"effect_source\\\": \\\"unknown_structural\\\",\\n        \\\"point_flip_threshold_pp\\\": 2.5,\\n        \\\"large_enough_to_flip\\\": null,\\n        \\\"flip_verdict\\\": \\\"UNKNOWN_BLOCKING\\\",\\n        \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high_that_it_is_a_confound_low_that_magnitude_is_known\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"KL placement changes the effective objective; no calibrated bound was supplied.\\\",\\n      \\\"recommended_action\\\": \\\"Rerun with identical KL placement, reference checkpoint, beta schedule, and estimator.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"D003\\\",\\n      \\\"lever\\\": {\\n        \\\"code\\\": \\\"MRR-3.rollout\\\",\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"Sampler, backend, and precision\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"paths\\\": [\\n        \\\"rollout.backend\\\",\\n        \\\"rollout.sampler_logit_precision\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"kind\\\": \\\"VALUE_DIFF\\\",\\n      \\\"role\\\": \\\"STACK_CONFOUND\\\",\\n      \\\"left\\\": {\\n        \\\"backend\\\": \\\"managed_api\\\",\\n        \\\"sampler_logit_precision\\\": \\\"unknown\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"right\\\": {\\n        \\\"backend\\\": \\\"vllm\\\",\\n        \\\"sampler_logit_precision\\\": \\\"bf16\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"materiality\\\": {\\n        \\\"risk\\\": \\\"critical\\\",\\n        \\\"effect_interval_pp\\\": {\\n          \\\"lower\\\": 5.0,\\n          \\\"upper\\\": 80.0\\n        },\\n        \\\"effect_source\\\": \\\"calibration:backend_ablation_qwen3_gsm8k\\\",\\n        \\\"point_flip_threshold_pp\\\": 2.5,\\n        \\\"large_enough_to_flip\\\": true,\\n        \\\"flip_verdict\\\": \\\"FLIPS_POINT\\\",\\n        \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Rollout backend defines the distribution consumed by the group-relative update.\\\",\\n      \\\"recommended_action\\\": \\\"Use the same rollout engine, decoding parameters, tokenizer, chat template, and sampler precision.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"rerun_recipe\\\": [\\n    \\\"Hold all non-allowed Min-Report-RL fields identical.\\\",\\n    \\\"Use one held-out split and one evaluation harness.\\\",\\n    \\\"Report per-step ZVF/Gu for both runs.\\\",\\n    \\\"Run parser and train/test contamination probes.\\\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7. Exit codes for CI and paper review\\n\\n```text\\n0  No material uncontrolled differences.\\n1  Differences exist, but calibrated bounds are below flip threshold.\\n2  At least one uncontrolled difference is large enough to flip the point comparison.\\n3  At least one mandatory field is missing or closed/unknown, blocking audit.\\n4  Evaluation or reward metric is incomparable.\\n5  Invalid manifest.\\n```\\n\\nA paper-review workflow could therefore run:\\n\\n```bash\\ngrpo-stackdiff diff claimed_variant.yaml baseline.yaml --strict\\n```\\n\\nand fail the check unless the only material differences are listed as intended method deltas.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8. Core philosophy\\n\\n`grpo-stackdiff` should be conservative in one specific way: it must never convert missing stack telemetry into “safe.” If a mandatory flip lever is absent, the output is not “no diff”; it is `REPORTING_GAP` or `UNKNOWN_BLOCKING`.\\n\\nThat matches the paper’s central claim: the field cannot audit what it does not record, and clean GRPO-family comparisons require the stack to be reported and held fixed except for the named variant delta. fileciteturn1file10\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a379b6e-4870-83e8-ac20-223ef5138950\",\"title\":\"MIN-REPORT-RL Plugin Design\",\"current_node\":\"1bb56b22-71e2-46fb-bf81-5f04cd7b1fab\",\"n_nodes\":37,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"ct\":\"text\",\"len\":40704,\"t\":\"Read the attached MIN-REPORT-RL position paper. Invent a TRL plugin that automatically emits the 7-item minimum-reportable-stack block as JSON at the start of every GRPO run and logs per-step ZVF/GU. Specify the hooks, config flags, and output schema.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-621\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | \\n512 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n513 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n514 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n515 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n516 | appendix.\\n517 | \\n518 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n519 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n520 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n521 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n522 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n523 | \\n524 | % =============================================================================\\n525 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n526 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n527 | % =============================================================================\\n528 | \\n529 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n530 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n531 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n532 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n533 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n534 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n535 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n536 | \\n537 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n538 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n539 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n540 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n541 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n542 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n543 | justify it the same way.}\\n544 | \\n545 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n546 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n547 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n548 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n549 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n550 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n551 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n552 | \\n553 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n554 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n555 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n556 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n557 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n558 | algorithm.\\n559 | \\n560 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n561 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n562 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n563 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n564 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n565 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n566 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n567 | reported.\\n568 | \\n569 | % =============================================================================\\n570 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n571 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n572 | % =============================================================================\\n573 | \\n574 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n575 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n576 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n577 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n578 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n579 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n580 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n581 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n582 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n583 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n584 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n585 | can actually trust.\\n586 | \\n587 | % =============================================================================\\n588 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n589 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n590 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n591 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n592 | %\\n593 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n594 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n595 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n596 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n597 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n598 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n599 | % =============================================================================\\n600 | \\\\section*{References}\\n601 | \\\\small\\n602 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n603 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n604 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n605 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n606 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n607 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n608 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n609 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n610 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n611 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n612 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n613 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n614 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n615 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n616 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n617 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n618 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n619 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n620 | \\n621 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"code\",\"len\":193,\"t\":\"{\\\"search_query\\\":[{\\\"q\\\":\\\"Hugging Face TRL GRPOTrainer docs callbacks logging rewards group rewards\\\"},{\\\"q\\\":\\\"Hugging Face TRL GRPOTrainer source callbacks log metrics\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"medium\\\"}\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"code\",\"len\":97,\"t\":\"{\\\"open\\\":[{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn917308search0\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn917308search3\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"long\\\"}\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"code\",\"len\":386,\"t\":\"{\\\"find\\\":[{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn390820view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"num_generations\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn390820view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"use_vllm\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn390820view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"per_device_train_batch_size\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn390820view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"mask_truncated_completions\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn390820view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"temperature\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn390820view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"GRPOConfig\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"medium\\\"}\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"code\",\"len\":66,\"t\":\"{\\\"open\\\":[{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn917308search1\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"long\\\"}\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"code\",\"len\":336,\"t\":\"{\\\"find\\\":[{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn948583view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"def _generate_and_score_completions\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn948583view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"reward_per_func\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn948583view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"self._metrics\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn948583view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"def log\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn948583view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"def _prepare_inputs\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"medium\\\"}\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"code\",\"len\":131,\"t\":\"{\\\"open\\\":[{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/trl/main/trl/trainer/grpo_trainer.py\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"long\\\"}\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"code\",\"len\":317,\"t\":\"{\\\"find\\\":[{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn529674view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"def _generate\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn529674view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"def _prepare_inputs\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn529674view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"def compute_loss\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn529674view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"def log(\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn529674view0\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"rewards_per_func\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"long\\\"}\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"code\",\"len\":410,\"t\":\"{\\\"queries\\\":[\\\"+(Min-Report-RL) seven-item minimum-reportable-stack loss form reference KL sampler backend precision per-step Zvf Gu group-size held-out decontamination --QDF=1\\\",\\\"+(report_min_report_rl) TRL GRPOTrainer Zvf Gu seven fields adoption path --QDF=1\\\",\\\"Zvf Gu per-step trajectory group rewards fraction identical reward Gradient Utilization --QDF=1\\\"],\\\"source_filter\\\":[\\\"files_uploaded_in_conversation\\\"]}\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"text\",\"len\":19084,\"t\":\"## Plugin: `trl-min-report-rl`\\n\\n`trl-min-report-rl` is a small TRL extension for `GRPOTrainer` that emits a machine-readable Min-Report-RL manifest before the first rollout and logs usable-signal telemetry, especially ZVF/GU, for every optimizer step. The attached position paper’s required seven fields are loss form, reference/KL handling, sampler/backend/precision, per-step ZVF/GU, group-size schedule, held-out split, and decontamination/parser probes; it also explicitly proposes a TRL flag named `report_min_report_rl=True` that writes the seven-field block and logs the ZVF/GU trajectory. fileciteturn0file0 fileciteturn1file4 fileciteturn1file0\\n\\nCurrent TRL already has the right raw material: `GRPOConfig` exposes fields such as `num_generations`, `temperature`, `top_p`, `use_vllm`, `beta`, `epsilon`, `epsilon_high`, `importance_sampling_level`, `scale_rewards`, `loss_type`, and `mask_truncated_completions`, and TRL’s logging path supports `report_to` for trackers such as Trackio, W&B, and TensorBoard. citeturn724457view5turn390820view1 The one missing piece is an explicit reward-group hook: TRL computes and gathers per-completion rewards before group normalization, which is exactly where ZVF/GU should be computed. citeturn989954view4\\n\\n---\\n\\n## User-facing API\\n\\n```python\\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\\nfrom trl import GRPOConfig\\nfrom trl_min_report_rl import MinReportGRPOTrainer, MinReportRLConfig\\n\\nargs = GRPOConfig(\\n    output_dir=\\\"runs/qwen-grpo\\\",\\n    num_generations=8,\\n    loss_type=\\\"dapo\\\",\\n    beta=0.0,\\n    use_vllm=True,\\n    vllm_mode=\\\"colocate\\\",\\n    temperature=1.0,\\n    top_p=1.0,\\n    report_to=[\\\"wandb\\\"],\\n\\n    # Proposed upstream TRL flags\\n    report_min_report_rl=True,\\n    min_report_rl_mode=\\\"warn\\\",\\n    min_report_rl_log_every_step=True,\\n)\\n\\nmin_report = MinReportRLConfig(\\n    heldout_manifest=\\\"eval/gsm8k_heldout_manifest.json\\\",\\n    decontamination_manifest=\\\"eval/gsm8k_decontam_probe.json\\\",\\n    reward_parser_name=\\\"gsm8k_exact_answer_v3\\\",\\n    reward_parser_probe=\\\"eval/parser_probe_results.json\\\",\\n)\\n\\ntrainer = MinReportGRPOTrainer(\\n    model=\\\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\\\",\\n    args=args,\\n    reward_funcs=[reward_fn],\\n    train_dataset=load_dataset(\\\"gsm8k\\\", \\\"main\\\", split=\\\"train[:500]\\\"),\\n    eval_dataset=load_dataset(\\\"gsm8k\\\", \\\"main\\\", split=\\\"test[:500]\\\"),\\n    min_report_rl=min_report,\\n)\\n\\ntrainer.train()\\n```\\n\\nThe plugin writes:\\n\\n```text\\nruns/qwen-grpo/min_report_rl.json\\nruns/qwen-grpo/min_report_rl.telemetry.jsonl\\nruns/qwen-grpo/min_report_rl.summary.json\\n```\\n\\nThe first JSONL line is a `run_start` event containing the same seven-item manifest; all subsequent lines are `step_telemetry` events.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Hooks\\n\\n### 1. `on_grpo_init_end(trainer)`\\n\\nRuns after `GRPOTrainer.__init__` has resolved the model, tokenizer/processor, PEFT config, reward functions, vLLM/generation config, reference model, precision settings, and optimizer arguments.\\n\\nPurpose: collect all static stack fields that should not change during training.\\n\\nCaptured fields include model ID, model revision if available, tokenizer ID, tokenizer hash, chat template hash, reward function identities, LoRA target modules/rank, dtype, optimizer, sampler backend, decoding parameters, loss type, clip settings, KL/reference settings, and initial group size.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. `on_train_begin(trainer)`\\n\\nRuns before the first call to `_prepare_inputs` or `_generate_and_score_completions`.\\n\\nPurpose: emit the seven-item manifest before the first rollout. Only rank 0 writes files and experiment-tracker artifacts; all ranks receive the manifest hash through a barrier/broadcast.\\n\\nOutputs:\\n\\n```text\\nmin_report_rl.json\\nmin_report_rl.telemetry.jsonl line 1: {\\\"event\\\": \\\"run_start\\\", ...}\\ntrainer log/config key: min_report_rl/block\\n```\\n\\nIf `resume_from_checkpoint` is set, the plugin emits a new `run_start` event with:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"resume\\\": {\\n  \\\"resumed\\\": true,\\n  \\\"checkpoint\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n  \\\"previous_manifest_sha256\\\": \\\"...\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. `on_grpo_rewards_computed(...)`\\n\\nInserted immediately after global reward gathering and before advantage normalization. This is the critical hook.\\n\\nProposed signature:\\n\\n```python\\ndef on_grpo_rewards_computed(\\n    trainer,\\n    *,\\n    mode: str,                         # \\\"train\\\" or \\\"eval\\\"\\n    global_step: int,\\n    rollout_step: int,\\n    num_generations: int,              # G\\n    prompts: list,\\n    completions: list,\\n    completion_ids: list[list[int]],\\n    rewards_per_func: torch.Tensor,    # shape [num_prompts * G, num_reward_funcs]\\n    reward_func_names: list[str],\\n    reward_weights: torch.Tensor,\\n    unscorable_mask: torch.Tensor | None,\\n) -> MinReportStepStats:\\n    ...\\n```\\n\\nFor an external plugin without an upstream TRL hook, `MinReportGRPOTrainer` can subclass `GRPOTrainer` and override `_calculate_rewards` or `_generate_and_score_completions`. Upstream, the cleaner patch is one line near the existing reward-gather point: call `self.min_report_rl.on_grpo_rewards_computed(...)` after `rewards_per_func = gather(rewards_per_func)`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. `on_training_step_end(trainer, stats)`\\n\\nRuns after each optimizer step.\\n\\nPurpose: write one JSONL row per optimizer step. If TRL reuses a rollout across `steps_per_generation` or `num_iterations`, the plugin re-emits the cached ZVF/GU stats with:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"rollout_reused\\\": true\\n```\\n\\nThis matters because TRL can generate once and reuse/buffer completions across multiple updates; the plugin logs per optimizer step while also recording the rollout step that produced the rewards. TRL’s own source describes this buffering/reuse in `_prepare_inputs`. citeturn989954view1\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. `on_log(trainer, logs)`\\n\\nAdds aggregated scalar keys to normal TRL logs:\\n\\n```text\\nmin_report_rl/zvf\\nmin_report_rl/gu\\nmin_report_rl/group_reward_std_mean\\nmin_report_rl/group_reward_std_p50\\nmin_report_rl/mixed_group_count\\nmin_report_rl/zero_signal_group_count\\nmin_report_rl/unscorable_group_count\\nmin_report_rl/group_size\\n```\\n\\nThe JSONL file remains the authoritative per-step trace. Tracker logs may be averaged by TRL logging intervals; TRL’s current `log` method averages metrics stored in `_metrics`. citeturn989954view3\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 6. `on_evaluate(trainer, metrics)`\\n\\nUpdates held-out evidence in the summary file:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"heldout_split\\\": {\\n  \\\"status\\\": \\\"evaluated\\\",\\n  \\\"n\\\": 500,\\n  \\\"metric\\\": \\\"exact_match\\\",\\n  \\\"value\\\": 0.833,\\n  \\\"ci95\\\": [0.801, 0.865],\\n  \\\"harness\\\": \\\"lm-eval-gsm8k-v2\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe start-of-run manifest still exists even when no held-out evaluation is configured; in that case the field is explicit, not omitted:\\n\\n```json\\n\\\"status\\\": \\\"not_configured\\\"\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 7. `on_train_end(trainer)`\\n\\nWrites `min_report_rl.summary.json`, including manifest hash, telemetry hash, first-five-step collapse triage, final ZVF/GU summary, held-out evaluation status, decontamination status, and missing-field report.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Config flags\\n\\nProposed additions to `GRPOConfig`:\\n\\n```python\\nreport_min_report_rl: bool = True\\nmin_report_rl_mode: Literal[\\\"off\\\", \\\"warn\\\", \\\"enforce\\\"] = \\\"warn\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_output_dir: str | None = None\\nmin_report_rl_emit_to: list[Literal[\\\"file\\\", \\\"trainer_log\\\", \\\"wandb\\\", \\\"trackio\\\", \\\"tensorboard\\\", \\\"hub\\\"]] = [\\\"file\\\", \\\"trainer_log\\\"]\\nmin_report_rl_manifest_filename: str = \\\"min_report_rl.json\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_telemetry_filename: str = \\\"min_report_rl.telemetry.jsonl\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_summary_filename: str = \\\"min_report_rl.summary.json\\\"\\n\\n# ZVF/GU behavior\\nmin_report_rl_log_every_step: bool = True\\nmin_report_rl_zvf_atol: float = 0.0\\nmin_report_rl_zvf_rtol: float = 0.0\\nmin_report_rl_nan_policy: Literal[\\\"zero_signal\\\", \\\"drop_group\\\", \\\"separate\\\"] = \\\"zero_signal\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_dense_reward_mode: Literal[\\\"auto\\\", \\\"zvf\\\", \\\"reward_variance\\\"] = \\\"auto\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_group_by: Literal[\\\"prompt_index\\\", \\\"prompt_hash\\\"] = \\\"prompt_index\\\"\\n\\n# Privacy and hashes\\nmin_report_rl_privacy: Literal[\\\"hash_only\\\", \\\"redact\\\", \\\"raw\\\"] = \\\"hash_only\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_hash_prompts: bool = True\\nmin_report_rl_hash_datasets: bool = True\\nmin_report_rl_hash_reward_funcs: bool = True\\nmin_report_rl_hash_chat_template: bool = True\\n\\n# Held-out and decontamination\\nmin_report_rl_require_heldout: bool = False\\nmin_report_rl_heldout_manifest: str | None = None\\nmin_report_rl_require_decontamination: bool = False\\nmin_report_rl_decontamination_manifest: str | None = None\\nmin_report_rl_reward_parser_name: str | None = None\\nmin_report_rl_reward_parser_version: str | None = None\\nmin_report_rl_parser_probe_results: str | None = None\\n\\n# Resume behavior\\nmin_report_rl_emit_on_resume: Literal[\\\"append\\\", \\\"overwrite_if_same\\\", \\\"always_new\\\"] = \\\"append\\\"\\n```\\n\\nRecommended defaults:\\n\\n```python\\nreport_min_report_rl=True\\nmin_report_rl_mode=\\\"warn\\\"\\nmin_report_rl_log_every_step=True\\nmin_report_rl_privacy=\\\"hash_only\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`warn` mode emits the manifest even when fields are missing. `enforce` mode raises before training if mandatory stack fields are unknown, except held-out/decontamination fields can be allowed as `not_configured` unless their `require_*` flag is true.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ZVF/GU computation\\n\\nThe plugin computes ZVF from scalar rewards after reward parser execution and reward weighting, but before advantage normalization.\\n\\nFor a step with `N` prompts and group size `G`, reshape scalar rewards as:\\n\\n```python\\ngroup_rewards = rewards.view(N, G)\\n```\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```python\\nvalid = ~torch.isnan(group_rewards)\\nvalid_count = valid.sum(dim=1)\\n\\ngroup_min = torch.nanmin(group_rewards, dim=1).values\\ngroup_max = torch.nanmax(group_rewards, dim=1).values\\nreward_span = group_max - group_min\\n\\nzero_signal = (\\n    (valid_count < 2) |\\n    (reward_span <= zvf_atol + zvf_rtol * torch.nanmean(torch.abs(group_rewards), dim=1))\\n)\\n\\nzvf = zero_signal.float().mean()\\ngu = 1.0 - zvf\\n```\\n\\nThe default `nan_policy=\\\"zero_signal\\\"` treats unscorable groups as zero-signal, because they cannot supply usable group-relative advantage. The plugin also logs `zvf_strict_identical_valid_only`, which excludes groups with fewer than two valid rewards, so papers can distinguish parser failures from true all-identical reward groups.\\n\\nFor dense process rewards, `dense_reward_mode=\\\"auto\\\"` switches the reported usable-signal metric to group reward variance when almost no groups are exactly identical. This follows the paper’s caveat that ZVF/GU is the default usable-signal trajectory, but dense process rewards may require reward-variance or gradient-variance substitutes. fileciteturn1file9\\n\\n---\\n\\n## `min_report_rl.json` output schema\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": \\\"min-report-rl/0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"kind\\\": \\\"grpo_run_manifest\\\",\\n  \\\"created_at_utc\\\": \\\"2026-06-21T08:05:52Z\\\",\\n  \\\"run\\\": {\\n    \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"uuid-or-tracker-run-id\\\",\\n    \\\"trainer\\\": \\\"trl.GRPOTrainer\\\",\\n    \\\"plugin\\\": \\\"trl-min-report-rl\\\",\\n    \\\"plugin_version\\\": \\\"0.1.0\\\",\\n    \\\"trl_version\\\": \\\"1.x\\\",\\n    \\\"transformers_version\\\": \\\"x.y.z\\\",\\n    \\\"accelerate_version\\\": \\\"x.y.z\\\",\\n    \\\"python_version\\\": \\\"3.12\\\",\\n    \\\"distributed\\\": {\\n      \\\"world_size\\\": 8,\\n      \\\"rank0_only_writes\\\": true,\\n      \\\"deepspeed\\\": null,\\n      \\\"fsdp\\\": null\\n    },\\n    \\\"seed\\\": 42,\\n    \\\"data_seed\\\": 42,\\n    \\\"output_dir\\\": \\\"runs/qwen-grpo\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"min_report_stack\\\": {\\n    \\\"1_loss_form\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"complete\\\",\\n      \\\"algorithm_label\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n      \\\"loss_type\\\": \\\"dapo\\\",\\n      \\\"uses_importance_ratio\\\": true,\\n      \\\"importance_sampling_level\\\": \\\"token\\\",\\n      \\\"num_iterations_per_rollout\\\": 1,\\n      \\\"clip\\\": {\\n        \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n        \\\"epsilon_low\\\": 0.2,\\n        \\\"epsilon_high\\\": 0.28,\\n        \\\"delta\\\": null\\n      },\\n      \\\"token_mask\\\": {\\n        \\\"policy_loss_mask\\\": \\\"completion_only\\\",\\n        \\\"mask_truncated_completions\\\": false,\\n        \\\"top_entropy_quantile\\\": 1.0\\n      },\\n      \\\"advantage_normalization\\\": {\\n        \\\"scale_rewards\\\": \\\"group\\\",\\n        \\\"multi_objective_aggregation\\\": \\\"sum_then_normalize\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"source_fields\\\": [\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.loss_type\\\",\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.epsilon\\\",\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.epsilon_high\\\",\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.importance_sampling_level\\\",\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.scale_rewards\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n\\n    \\\"2_reference_policy_and_kl\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"complete\\\",\\n      \\\"reference_policy\\\": {\\n        \\\"retained\\\": false,\\n        \\\"mode\\\": \\\"none_when_beta_0\\\",\\n        \\\"sync_ref_model\\\": false,\\n        \\\"ref_model_sync_steps\\\": null,\\n        \\\"ref_model_mixup_alpha\\\": null\\n      },\\n      \\\"kl\\\": {\\n        \\\"placement\\\": \\\"none\\\",\\n        \\\"beta\\\": 0.0,\\n        \\\"schedule\\\": \\\"constant\\\",\\n        \\\"estimator\\\": \\\"forward_kl_approximation\\\",\\n        \\\"granularity\\\": \\\"per_token\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"source_fields\\\": [\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.beta\\\",\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.sync_ref_model\\\",\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.ref_model_sync_steps\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n\\n    \\\"3_sampler_backend_precision\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"partial\\\",\\n      \\\"backend\\\": {\\n        \\\"engine\\\": \\\"vllm\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_mode\\\": \\\"colocate\\\",\\n        \\\"vllm_model_impl\\\": \\\"vllm\\\",\\n        \\\"custom_rollout_func\\\": false\\n      },\\n      \\\"decoding\\\": {\\n        \\\"num_generations\\\": 8,\\n        \\\"max_completion_length\\\": 256,\\n        \\\"temperature\\\": 1.0,\\n        \\\"top_p\\\": 1.0,\\n        \\\"top_k\\\": 0,\\n        \\\"min_p\\\": null,\\n        \\\"repetition_penalty\\\": 1.0\\n      },\\n      \\\"precision\\\": {\\n        \\\"trainer_dtype\\\": \\\"bf16\\\",\\n        \\\"sampler_logit_precision\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n        \\\"tf32\\\": true,\\n        \\\"cast_lm_head_to_fp32\\\": false\\n      },\\n      \\\"tokenizer_and_template\\\": {\\n        \\\"model_id\\\": \\\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\\\",\\n        \\\"tokenizer_id\\\": \\\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\\\",\\n        \\\"tokenizer_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:...\\\",\\n        \\\"chat_template_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:...\\\",\\n        \\\"sampler_trainer_template_shared\\\": true\\n      },\\n      \\\"missing\\\": [\\n        \\\"sampler_logit_precision\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n\\n    \\\"4_zvf_gu_trajectory\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"complete\\\",\\n      \\\"enabled\\\": true,\\n      \\\"telemetry_file\\\": \\\"min_report_rl.telemetry.jsonl\\\",\\n      \\\"metrics\\\": {\\n        \\\"zvf\\\": \\\"fraction of prompt groups with zero usable reward variance\\\",\\n        \\\"gu\\\": \\\"1 - zvf\\\",\\n        \\\"group_reward_std\\\": \\\"std of scalar rewards within each prompt group\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"zvf_definition\\\": {\\n        \\\"computed_on\\\": \\\"weighted_scalar_rewards_before_advantage_normalization\\\",\\n        \\\"nan_policy\\\": \\\"zero_signal\\\",\\n        \\\"atol\\\": 0.0,\\n        \\\"rtol\\\": 0.0,\\n        \\\"unscorable_groups_count_as_zero_signal\\\": true\\n      },\\n      \\\"collapse_triage\\\": {\\n        \\\"first_five_rule_enabled\\\": true,\\n        \\\"collapse_if_zvf_ge\\\": 0.8,\\n        \\\"and_reward_le\\\": 0.05\\n      }\\n    },\\n\\n    \\\"5_group_size_schedule\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"complete\\\",\\n      \\\"initial_group_size\\\": 8,\\n      \\\"schedule_type\\\": \\\"fixed\\\",\\n      \\\"adaptive_rule\\\": null,\\n      \\\"per_step_logged\\\": true,\\n      \\\"source_fields\\\": [\\n        \\\"GRPOConfig.num_generations\\\"\\n      ]\\n    },\\n\\n    \\\"6_heldout_split\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_configured\\\",\\n      \\\"train_eval_disjoint\\\": \\\"unknown\\\",\\n      \\\"heldout_dataset\\\": null,\\n      \\\"heldout_split\\\": null,\\n      \\\"sample_size\\\": null,\\n      \\\"metric\\\": null,\\n      \\\"confidence_interval\\\": null,\\n      \\\"harness\\\": null,\\n      \\\"manifest\\\": null\\n    },\\n\\n    \\\"7_decontamination_probe\\\": {\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"not_configured\\\",\\n      \\\"train_test_overlap_probe\\\": {\\n        \\\"method\\\": null,\\n        \\\"result\\\": null,\\n        \\\"manifest\\\": null\\n      },\\n      \\\"parser_probe\\\": {\\n        \\\"reward_parser_name\\\": \\\"gsm8k_exact_answer_v3\\\",\\n        \\\"reward_parser_version\\\": null,\\n        \\\"format_only_probe_result\\\": null,\\n        \\\"manifest\\\": null\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"capture_quality\\\": {\\n    \\\"complete_items\\\": 5,\\n    \\\"partial_items\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"not_configured_items\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"missing_required_if_enforce\\\": [\\n      \\\"sampler_logit_precision\\\",\\n      \\\"heldout_split.manifest\\\",\\n      \\\"decontamination_probe.manifest\\\"\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \\\"manifest_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:...\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## `min_report_rl.telemetry.jsonl` schema\\n\\nFirst line:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": \\\"min-report-rl/telemetry/0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"event\\\": \\\"run_start\\\",\\n  \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"uuid-or-tracker-run-id\\\",\\n  \\\"global_step\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"manifest_sha256\\\": \\\"sha256:...\\\",\\n  \\\"manifest_path\\\": \\\"min_report_rl.json\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\nPer-step line:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"schema\\\": \\\"min-report-rl/telemetry/0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"event\\\": \\\"step_telemetry\\\",\\n  \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"uuid-or-tracker-run-id\\\",\\n  \\\"mode\\\": \\\"train\\\",\\n  \\\"global_step\\\": 17,\\n  \\\"optimizer_step\\\": 17,\\n  \\\"rollout_step\\\": 15,\\n  \\\"rollout_reused\\\": false,\\n  \\\"group_size\\\": 8,\\n  \\\"num_prompt_groups\\\": 64,\\n  \\\"num_completions\\\": 512,\\n\\n  \\\"zvf\\\": 0.3125,\\n  \\\"gu\\\": 0.6875,\\n  \\\"zvf_strict_identical_valid_only\\\": 0.296875,\\n  \\\"zero_signal_group_count\\\": 20,\\n  \\\"mixed_group_count\\\": 44,\\n  \\\"unscorable_group_count\\\": 1,\\n\\n  \\\"reward\\\": {\\n    \\\"mean\\\": 0.421,\\n    \\\"std\\\": 0.494,\\n    \\\"group_std_mean\\\": 0.318,\\n    \\\"group_std_p50\\\": 0.353,\\n    \\\"group_std_min\\\": 0.0,\\n    \\\"group_std_max\\\": 0.535\\n  },\\n\\n  \\\"reward_functions\\\": {\\n    \\\"accuracy_reward\\\": {\\n      \\\"mean\\\": 0.421,\\n      \\\"std\\\": 0.494,\\n      \\\"nan_count\\\": 0\\n    }\\n  },\\n\\n  \\\"collapse_triage\\\": {\\n    \\\"first_five_window\\\": false,\\n    \\\"collapse_flag\\\": false,\\n    \\\"reason\\\": null\\n  },\\n\\n  \\\"privacy\\\": {\\n    \\\"prompt_logging\\\": \\\"hash_only\\\",\\n    \\\"prompt_hash_salt\\\": \\\"run_salt_sha256:...\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Minimal upstream patch shape\\n\\n```python\\n# trl/trainer/grpo_config.py\\n@dataclass\\nclass GRPOConfig(...):\\n    report_min_report_rl: bool = True\\n    min_report_rl_mode: str = \\\"warn\\\"\\n    min_report_rl_log_every_step: bool = True\\n    min_report_rl_output_dir: str | None = None\\n    min_report_rl_zvf_atol: float = 0.0\\n    min_report_rl_zvf_rtol: float = 0.0\\n    min_report_rl_nan_policy: str = \\\"zero_signal\\\"\\n```\\n\\n```python\\n# trl/trainer/grpo_trainer.py\\nclass GRPOTrainer(...):\\n    def __init__(..., min_report_rl: MinReportRLConfig | None = None, ...):\\n        ...\\n        self.min_report_rl = maybe_build_min_report_plugin(args, min_report_rl)\\n        self.min_report_rl.on_grpo_init_end(self)\\n\\n    def train(...):\\n        self.min_report_rl.on_train_begin(self)\\n        return super().train(...)\\n\\n    def _calculate_rewards(...):\\n        rewards_per_func = super_reward_calculation(...)\\n        self.min_report_rl.on_grpo_rewards_computed(\\n            self,\\n            mode=\\\"train\\\" if self.model.training else \\\"eval\\\",\\n            global_step=self.state.global_step,\\n            num_generations=self.num_generations if self.model.training else self.num_generations_eval,\\n            prompts=prompts,\\n            completions=completions,\\n            completion_ids=completion_ids_list,\\n            rewards_per_func=rewards_per_func,\\n            reward_func_names=self.reward_func_names,\\n            reward_weights=self.reward_weights,\\n        )\\n        return rewards_per_func\\n\\n    def training_step(...):\\n        out = super().training_step(...)\\n        self.min_report_rl.on_training_step_end(self)\\n        return out\\n\\n    def log(self, logs, start_time=None):\\n        self.min_report_rl.on_log(self, logs)\\n        return super().log(logs, start_time)\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Enforcement behavior\\n\\nIn `warn` mode, the plugin never blocks training. It emits the JSON with explicit `unknown`, `partial`, or `not_configured` statuses.\\n\\nIn `enforce` mode, it blocks before the first rollout if items 1–5 are incomplete, because those are directly available inside TRL. It blocks on items 6–7 only when `min_report_rl_require_heldout=True` or `min_report_rl_require_decontamination=True`.\\n\\nThat gives the paper exactly what it asks for: the cost is a JSON block plus a per-step usable-signal trace, while the implementation stays local to the existing GRPO reward/logging path.\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a37a843-e158-83e8-9139-05719599a47c\",\"title\":\"Automated Compliance Checker\",\"current_node\":\"b2992556-fd43-4e14-b12e-e1e0f3e43426\",\"n_nodes\":13,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"ct\":\"text\",\"len\":51323,\"t\":\"Read the attached paper. Invent an automated compliance checker that scans a GRPO paper or repo and scores it against the 7-item MIN-REPORT-RL checklist. Provide the scoring rubric, heuristics for each item, and example outputs.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-621\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | \\n512 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n513 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n514 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n515 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n516 | appendix.\\n517 | \\n518 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n519 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n520 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n521 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n522 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n523 | \\n524 | % =============================================================================\\n525 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n526 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n527 | % =============================================================================\\n528 | \\n529 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n530 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n531 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n532 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n533 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n534 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n535 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n536 | \\n537 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n538 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n539 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n540 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n541 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n542 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n543 | justify it the same way.}\\n544 | \\n545 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n546 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n547 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n548 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n549 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n550 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n551 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n552 | \\n553 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n554 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n555 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n556 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n557 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n558 | algorithm.\\n559 | \\n560 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n561 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n562 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n563 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n564 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n565 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n566 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n567 | reported.\\n568 | \\n569 | % =============================================================================\\n570 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n571 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n572 | % =============================================================================\\n573 | \\n574 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n575 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n576 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n577 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n578 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n579 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n580 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n581 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n582 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n583 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n584 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n585 | can actually trust.\\n586 | \\n587 | % =============================================================================\\n588 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n589 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n590 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n591 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n592 | %\\n593 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n594 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n595 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n596 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n597 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n598 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n599 | % =============================================================================\\n600 | \\\\section*{References}\\n601 | \\\\small\\n602 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n603 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n604 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n605 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n606 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n607 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n608 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n609 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n610 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n611 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n612 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n613 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n614 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n615 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n616 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n617 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n618 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n619 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n620 | \\n621 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/CHECKLIST.md\\nLines: 1-168\\n````md\\n  1 | # MIN-REPORT-RL — Author Checklist\\n  2 | \\n  3 | A copy-pasteable minimum-reportable-stack for **any GRPO-family RL post-training paper**\\n  4 | (GRPO, DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO, …).\\n  5 | \\n  6 | **Why this exists.** Algorithm labels are under-specified treatments. In a controlled audit,\\n  7 | a *nominally identical* GRPO config (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset, seed,\\n  8 | step budget) produced **84.4%** [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] last-10 training reward on one backend and **5.0%** [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] on\\n  9 | another — a ~17× gap [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] with **no visible hyperparameter difference**. The label was constant;\\n 10 | the stack was not. Each item below is on the list because it is a **documented lever that can\\n 11 | flip a head-to-head comparison**. If two papers both report these seven fields, a reader can\\n 12 | tell whether their comparison is confounded.\\n 13 | \\n 14 | Report all seven. They are a *minimum*, not a maximum.\\n 15 | \\n 16 | **Provenance / scope of the worked numbers in this checklist.** The concrete\\n 17 | numbers used as motivating examples below (61.6–89.6% prompt-token\\n 18 | loss magnitude; the 17× matched-config gap; the 82.0→83.3% held-out\\n 19 | control at p=0.26; the 95.0–98.1% / 95.0% Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n 20 | are inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this position\\n 21 | piece. **[TODO:trace to v1 audit citation]** A reader who wants to\\n 22 | verify the numbers should consult that paper; this checklist presents\\n 23 | them as illustrative of the *kind* of stack-driven flip, not as\\n 24 | re-derivable from a fresh run. Once the v1 audit paper is in\\n 25 | citation scope, the references go in the `\\\\bibliography{...}` and\\n 26 | the [TODO:trace] markers are replaced with real `\\\\cite{}` keys.\\n 27 | \\n 28 | ---\\n 29 | \\n 30 | ## The 7 items\\n 31 | \\n 32 | ### 1. Loss form\\n 33 | - **Report:** PPO importance ratio used? (yes/no). Clipped? bounds (incl. asymmetric\\n 34 |   \\\"clip-higher\\\")? Token mask: completion-only or whole-sequence? Advantage normalization:\\n 35 |   per-group / per-batch / running estimate?\\n 36 | - **Why it can flip:** the token mask reassigns gradient. In one diagnostic [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation], **61.6–89.6%**\\n 37 |   of full-sequence loss magnitude came from *prompt* tokens, not completion tokens — a\\n 38 |   whole-sequence and a completion-only mask are different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO\\n 39 |   *is* a normalization change; GSPO *is* an IS-granularity change. If the baseline loss form\\n 40 |   is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n 41 | - **Good:** \\\"Token-masked completion-only; PPO ratio with symmetric clip ε=0.2; per-group\\n 42 |   advantage normalization (subtract group mean, divide by group std).\\\"\\n 43 | - **Bad:** \\\"We use the standard GRPO loss.\\\"\\n 44 | \\n 45 | ### 2. Reference policy + KL handling\\n 46 | - **Report:** frozen reference policy retained? (yes/no). KL term in the **loss** or folded\\n 47 |   into the **reward**? KL coefficient + schedule. Forward/reverse, per-token/per-sequence.\\n 48 | - **Why it can flip:** KL placement changes the objective and steady-state exploration. A\\n 49 |   runner with *no* frozen reference (one optimizer step per rollout) is not doing KL-regularized\\n 50 |   GRPO even at matched LR. Dual-anchor methods shift steady-state ZVF and must be recalibrated.\\n 51 | - **Good:** \\\"Frozen reference = SFT checkpoint; KL in the loss, β=0.04 constant; reverse KL,\\n 52 |   per-token.\\\"\\n 53 | - **Bad:** \\\"KL regularization as usual.\\\" / (silent on whether a reference exists)\\n 54 | \\n 55 | ### 3. Sampler / backend / precision\\n 56 | - **Report:** rollout engine (vLLM / SGLang / managed API / trainer `.generate`). Decoding\\n 57 |   params (temperature, top-p, max_tokens). Logit precision in sampler vs. trainer\\n 58 |   (bf16/fp16/fp32). Same tokenizer + chat template in sampler and trainer? (yes/no).\\n 59 | - **Why it can flip:** the sampler *defines* the rollout distribution the group-relative update\\n 60 |   consumes. A managed runner vs. an open vLLM path gave the **17×** [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] matched-config gap. Sampler\\n 61 |   precision shifts the probability of mixed-reward groups, hence available gradient.\\n 62 | - **Good:** \\\"vLLM 0.x rollouts, bf16; trainer logits bf16; temp 0.8, top-p 1.0, max_tokens 512;\\n 63 |   identical tokenizer/chat template across both.\\\"\\n 64 | - **Bad:** \\\"Generated with default settings.\\\"\\n 65 | \\n 66 | ### 4. Per-step ZVF and GU trajectory\\n 67 | - **Report:** per-step **ZVF** (fraction of prompts whose G completions all get identical\\n 68 |   reward → zero gradient) and **GU = 1 − ZVF**, logged every step (release the trajectory).\\n 69 | - **Why it can flip:** ZVF/GU reveals whether the run had *any* usable learning signal. A\\n 70 |   method can \\\"win\\\" purely because its stack produced lower ZVF (more usable groups), not because\\n 71 |   its algorithm is better. Without the trajectory, a collapsed run and a saturated run look\\n 72 |   identical from final reward. (Mixed-group probability:\\n 73 |   `P(usable) ≈ (1/N) Σ_x [1 − (1−p_x)^G − p_x^G]`.) Cheap collapse triage: first-5-step rule\\n 74 |   ZVF ≥ 80% with reward ≤ 5%.\\n 75 | - **Good:** \\\"ZVF/GU logged per step (released CSV); mean ZVF@25 = 0.43; no run trips the\\n 76 |   collapse rule.\\\"\\n 77 | - **Bad:** Only final reward reported; no per-step signal telemetry.\\n 78 | - **Substitutes allowed:** under dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration ZVF\\n 79 |   degenerates → report per-step reward variance or gradient-norm variance instead. Item 4\\n 80 |   mandates *a usable-signal trajectory*, of which ZVF/GU is the default.\\n 81 | \\n 82 | ### 5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive)\\n 83 | - **Report:** group size G at every step; the rule that changes it if adaptive (e.g. AERO-style\\n 84 |   double/halve on rolling ZVF).\\n 85 | - **Why it can flip:** G sets the mixed-group probability and was one of the strongest single\\n 86 |   knobs measured (G = 2→4→8→16 moved last-10 reward across a wide band). Adaptive-G vs. fixed-G\\n 87 |   partly credits the variant for spending more compute on hard prompts — separate that from the\\n 88 |   algorithmic claim.\\n 89 | - **Good:** \\\"Fixed G=8 throughout.\\\" / \\\"Adaptive G∈{4,…,16}, baseline 8, double when rolling\\n 90 |   ZVF>0.8, halve when <0.3, window 10.\\\"\\n 91 | - **Bad:** \\\"Group size 8.\\\" (when it was actually adaptive)\\n 92 | \\n 93 | ### 6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment\\n 94 | - **Report:** a held-out slice **disjoint** from training prompts, scored by a harness, with N\\n 95 |   and a CI, reported **separately** from online training reward.\\n 96 | - **Why it can flip:** training reward is dynamics, not capability. A clean paired control\\n 97 |   improved only **82.0% → 83.3% (p=0.26)** [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] despite near-saturated training reward; selecting\\n 98 |   checkpoints by training reward produced a spurious 87–95% \\\"capability\\\" band. Four 70B seeds\\n 99 |   ranged 95.0–98.1% on training last-10 [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation] yet all landed on 95.0% held-out (sampling noise, not\\n100 |   generalization).\\n101 | - **Good:** \\\"Held-out = 500 GSM8K test problems, seed 0, disjoint from every training batch;\\n102 |   base 82.0% → post 83.3% [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation], Wilson 95% CI, paired per-prompt p=0.54 [TODO:trace to v1 audit citation].\\\"</update>\\n103 | - **Bad:** Reporting training-set reward as the capability number; \\\"accuracy 94%\\\" with no split\\n104 |   stated.\\n105 | \\n106 | ### 7. Decontamination probe results\\n107 | - **Report:** train/test contamination check (n-gram or embedding overlap between training\\n108 |   prompts and the held-out/benchmark slice) AND parser behavior on adversarial format-only\\n109 |   inputs.\\n110 | - **Why it can flip:** verifiable rewards still admit reward hacking — parser artifacts, format\\n111 |   shortcuts, length effects, train-prompt overfitting. Overlap → \\\"gain\\\" may be memorization;\\n112 |   parser rewards a format token → \\\"gain\\\" may be a shortcut.\\n113 | - **Good:** \\\"Max 8-gram overlap between train and held-out = 0.0%; parser rejects 100% of\\n114 |   format-only (no-answer) adversarial inputs.\\\"\\n115 | - **Bad:** No contamination check; parser behavior unstated.\\n116 | \\n117 | ---\\n118 | \\n119 | ## Fillable appendix template (drop into your paper)\\n120 | \\n121 | ```\\n122 | ================ MIN-REPORT-RL BLOCK ================\\n123 | Method label:            <e.g. GRPO / DAPO / GSPO / ...>\\n124 | Base checkpoint:         <model id + revision/hash>\\n125 | Tokenizer + chat tmpl:   <id; same in sampler and trainer? yes/no>\\n126 | \\n127 | [1] LOSS FORM\\n128 |     PPO ratio used:      <yes/no>\\n129 |     Clip:                <none / symmetric eps=__ / asymmetric lo=__ hi=__>\\n130 |     Token mask:          <completion-only / whole-sequence>\\n131 |     Advantage norm.:     <per-group / per-batch / running; formula>\\n132 | \\n133 | [2] REFERENCE + KL\\n134 |     Frozen reference:    <yes (=__ checkpoint) / no>\\n135 |     KL location:         <in loss / in reward / absent>\\n136 |     KL coeff + schedule: <beta=__, schedule=__>\\n137 |     KL estimator:        <forward/reverse, per-token/per-seq>\\n138 | \\n139 | [3] SAMPLER / BACKEND / PRECISION\\n140 |     Rollout engine:      <vLLM/SGLang/managed API/trainer.generate; version>\\n141 |     Decoding:            <temp=__, top_p=__, max_tokens=__>\\n142 |     Precision:           <sampler=__, trainer=__>\\n143 | \\n144 | [4] ZVF / GU TRAJECTORY\\n145 |     Per-step logged:     <yes/no; artifact link>\\n146 |     Mean ZVF@25:         <__>      Mean GU@25: <__>\\n147 |     Collapse rule trip:  <__ / S seeds>   (rule: ZVF>=80% & reward<=5% in first 5 steps)\\n148 |     (or substitute metric under dense/scaffolded rewards: __________)\\n149 | \\n150 | [5] GROUP-SIZE SCHEDULE\\n151 |     Type:                <fixed G=__ / adaptive (rule=__, bounds=__, window=__)>\\n152 | \\n153 | [6] HELD-OUT SPLIT\\n154 |     Slice:               <dataset/split, N=__, seed=__, disjoint from train? yes/no>\\n155 |     Base vs post:        <__% -> __%, CI=__, test=__ (p=__)>\\n156 |     Reported separately from training reward: <yes/no>\\n157 | \\n158 | [7] DECONTAMINATION\\n159 |     Train/test overlap:  <metric=__, value=__>\\n160 |     Parser adversarial:  <format-only reject rate=__%>\\n161 | ====================================================\\n162 | ```\\n163 | \\n164 | ---\\n165 | \\n166 | ## One-line self-test\\n167 | If a reader cannot reproduce your ranking of method A vs. method B **from the block above\\n168 | alone**, the block is incomplete — find the unreported lever and add it.\\n````\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"text\",\"len\":30057,\"t\":\"Below is an invented tool spec for **MIN-REPORT-RL Auditor**, an automated compliance checker for GRPO-family papers and repos. It is designed around the paper’s core claim: a GRPO label is not a complete experimental description, because loss form, KL/reference handling, sampler/backend/precision, ZVF/GU telemetry, group-size schedule, held-out evaluation, and decontamination can each change the result. fileciteturn0file0\\n\\n# MIN-REPORT-RL Auditor\\n\\n**Purpose:** scan a GRPO-family paper, appendix, repo, logs, and release artifacts; extract evidence for the 7-item MIN-REPORT-RL checklist; assign a reproducibility-compliance score; and produce a reviewer-ready explanation of what is missing.\\n\\n**Target methods:** GRPO, DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO, and close variants.\\n\\n**Inputs:**\\n\\n```bash\\nminreport-audit \\\\\\n  --paper paper.pdf \\\\\\n  --repo ./code_release \\\\\\n  --logs ./wandb_export_or_tensorboard \\\\\\n  --mode strict \\\\\\n  --out minreport_audit.json\\n```\\n\\n**Outputs:**\\n\\n1. `minreport_audit.json` — machine-readable score and evidence.\\n2. `MIN_REPORT_RL_REVIEW.md` — reviewer-facing summary.\\n3. `evidence/` — quoted paper spans, config snippets, code locations, log checks.\\n4. Optional badge: `MIN-REPORT-RL: Complete / Usable / Partial / Not Auditable`.\\n\\nThe checker should not merely ask whether an item is “mentioned.” It should ask whether the paper or repo reports enough concrete information for another reader to tell whether two GRPO stacks are comparable. That follows the paper’s definition of MIN-REPORT-RL as the smallest set of fields needed to detect confounding between GRPO-family comparisons. fileciteturn1file4\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 1. Scoring rubric\\n\\n## Overall score\\n\\nEach of the 7 checklist items receives **0–10 points**.\\n\\n```text\\nRaw score = sum(item scores), max = 70\\nNormalized score = round(100 * raw / 70)\\n```\\n\\n## Verdict bands\\n\\n| Normalized score | Badge | Meaning |\\n|---:|---|---|\\n| 90–100 | **Complete** | All seven fields are concrete, auditable, and consistent across paper/repo/logs. |\\n| 75–89 | **Usable with caveats** | A reviewer can mostly reconstruct the stack, but some subfields or artifacts are weak. |\\n| 50–74 | **Partial** | The paper gives useful details, but multiple flip levers remain under-specified. |\\n| 25–49 | **Not reproducibility-auditable** | Mentions GRPO/RL details, but not enough to distinguish algorithm from stack. |\\n| 0–24 | **Non-compliant** | Mostly label-only reporting. |\\n\\n## Per-item scoring scale\\n\\nFor every checklist item, the auditor assigns a score using this logic:\\n\\n| Score | Meaning |\\n|---:|---|\\n| 10 | Complete: all required subfields present, explicit, and supported by paper/repo/log evidence. |\\n| 8 | Mostly complete: one minor subfield missing, but the stack is still interpretable. |\\n| 5 | Partial: key facts are present, but an important ambiguity remains. |\\n| 2 | Mention-only: item is named but not specified enough to reproduce or audit. |\\n| 0 | Absent, contradictory, or actively misleading. |\\n\\n## Evidence confidence\\n\\nThe score and confidence are separate. A paper can score 8/10 with low confidence if the evidence comes from prose only and the repo contradicts it.\\n\\nEvidence classes:\\n\\n| Evidence class | Confidence effect |\\n|---|---|\\n| Canonical `MIN_REPORT_RL_BLOCK` plus matching repo/log artifacts | Highest |\\n| Explicit paper/appendix text with exact values | High |\\n| Config/code evidence with commit hash | High |\\n| Log-derived evidence | High for telemetry items |\\n| Inferred framework defaults | Low; never enough for full credit |\\n| “Standard GRPO,” “default settings,” “as usual” | Mention-only or zero |\\n\\n## Hard caps\\n\\nThese prevent superficial compliance.\\n\\n1. If the paper has no repo or artifact links, item 4, ZVF/GU trajectory, is capped at **5/10** unless the full trajectory is embedded in the appendix.\\n2. If held-out evaluation is not demonstrably disjoint from training prompts, item 6 is capped at **4/10**.\\n3. If contamination is only discussed conceptually but no probe result is reported, item 7 is capped at **3/10**.\\n4. If a closed/managed backend hides loss or KL internals, items 1–2 cannot exceed **4/10** unless the authors explicitly scope the claim as “platform implementation,” not generic GRPO. The paper itself makes this scoping point for closed runners. fileciteturn1file10\\n5. If paper and repo disagree, use the lower score and emit a contradiction warning.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 2. System design\\n\\n## Pipeline\\n\\n**Stage 1 — Ingest**\\n\\nThe auditor parses:\\n\\n```text\\npaper.pdf / paper.tex / appendix.md\\nREADME.md\\nconfigs/*.yaml, *.json, *.toml\\ntraining scripts\\nevaluation scripts\\nreward parser code\\ndecontamination scripts\\nlogs: wandb, tensorboard, CSV, JSONL\\nmodel cards / release notes\\n```\\n\\n**Stage 2 — Detect GRPO-family scope**\\n\\nIt looks for method labels and variants:\\n\\n```text\\nGRPO, DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO,\\nAERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO,\\ngroup relative, group-relative, num_generations,\\ngroup_size, per-group advantage, zero-variance fraction\\n```\\n\\n**Stage 3 — Evidence extraction**\\n\\nThe auditor combines:\\n\\n- regex dictionaries for common field names;\\n- AST/static-code analysis for Python training loops;\\n- config-key mapping for TRL, verl, OpenRLHF, custom trainers;\\n- log-schema detection;\\n- semantic extraction from prose and tables;\\n- consistency checks between paper, configs, and code.\\n\\n**Stage 4 — Normalization**\\n\\nAll extracted evidence is normalized into a canonical schema:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"method_label\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n  \\\"base_checkpoint\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n  \\\"tokenizer_chat_template\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n  \\\"loss_form\\\": {},\\n  \\\"reference_kl\\\": {},\\n  \\\"sampler_backend_precision\\\": {},\\n  \\\"zvf_gu_trajectory\\\": {},\\n  \\\"group_size_schedule\\\": {},\\n  \\\"heldout_split\\\": {},\\n  \\\"decontamination\\\": {}\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Stage 5 — Score and explain**\\n\\nFor every item, the auditor stores:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"score\\\": 8,\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": 0.83,\\n  \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"source\\\": \\\"paper\\\",\\n      \\\"location\\\": \\\"appendix B.2\\\",\\n      \\\"quote\\\": \\\"Token-masked completion-only...\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"source\\\": \\\"repo\\\",\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"configs/grpo.yaml\\\",\\n      \\\"keys\\\": [\\\"loss.mask=completion_only\\\", \\\"clip_eps=0.2\\\"]\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"missing\\\": [\\\"advantage normalization formula\\\"],\\n  \\\"warnings\\\": []\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3. Heuristics for each checklist item\\n\\nThe paper’s seven items are treated as mandatory because each is a flip lever: loss form, KL/reference, sampler/backend/precision, ZVF/GU, group-size schedule, held-out split, and decontamination. fileciteturn1file3\\n\\n## Item 1 — Loss form\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\nScore out of 10:\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| PPO-style importance ratio used or explicitly absent | 2 |\\n| Clipping status and bounds, including asymmetric clip-higher | 2 |\\n| Token mask: completion-only vs whole-sequence | 2 |\\n| Advantage normalization: per-group, per-batch, running, or formula | 2 |\\n| Baseline and variant loss forms both specified | 2 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\nimportance ratio, old logprobs, pi_theta / pi_old,\\nclip, epsilon, cliprange, clip-higher, asymmetric clip,\\ncompletion-only, token mask, loss mask, response mask,\\nwhole-sequence, prompt tokens,\\nadvantage normalization, group mean, group std,\\nper-group, per-batch, running estimate,\\nDr.GRPO, GSPO, DAPO\\n```\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nLook for config keys and code patterns:\\n\\n```text\\nclip_range, cliprange, clip_eps, epsilon, clip_high, clip_low\\nuse_ppo_ratio, importance_sampling, old_log_probs\\ncompletion_mask, response_mask, prompt_mask, loss_mask\\nadvantages = (rewards - rewards.mean(...)) / rewards.std(...)\\nnum_generations, group_rewards, normalize_advantages\\ntorch.exp(logp - old_logp)\\ntorch.clamp(ratio, 1-eps, 1+eps)\\n```\\n\\n**Static-code checks**\\n\\n- Detect whether `old_log_probs` are used.\\n- Detect whether the loss includes prompt tokens by tracing masks.\\n- Detect ratio clipping via `torch.clamp`, `minimum`, `maximum`, or custom clipping.\\n- Detect sequence-level vs token-level importance ratio for GSPO-like variants.\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- “We use the standard GRPO loss” earns at most **2/10**.\\n- “We follow TRL defaults” earns at most **3/10** unless the repo pins the TRL version and confirms the actual loss path.\\n- A formula in the method section earns partial credit only if the baseline and implementation are consistent.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Item 2 — Reference policy and KL handling\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Frozen reference policy retained or explicitly absent | 2 |\\n| Reference checkpoint/revision if retained | 1 |\\n| KL location: loss, reward, or absent | 2 |\\n| KL coefficient and schedule | 2 |\\n| KL estimator: forward/reverse, per-token/per-sequence | 2 |\\n| Paper/repo consistency | 1 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\nreference model, frozen reference, ref policy, old policy,\\nKL, KL penalty, KL regularization, beta, kl_coef,\\nin loss, in reward, reward shaping, non-score reward,\\nforward KL, reverse KL, per-token KL, sequence KL,\\nSFT anchor, dual KL, reference-free\\n```\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nLook for:\\n\\n```text\\nref_model, reference_model, create_reference_model\\nkl_coef, beta, init_kl_coef, target_kl\\nkl_penalty, kl_loss, non_score_reward\\nloss += beta * kl\\nreward -= beta * kl\\nref_model = None\\ndisable_reference, use_ref_model: false\\n```\\n\\n**Placement classifier**\\n\\nThe auditor distinguishes:\\n\\n```python\\nif kl appears inside reward computation:\\n    kl_location = \\\"reward\\\"\\nelif kl is added to objective/loss:\\n    kl_location = \\\"loss\\\"\\nelif beta == 0 or ref_model is None:\\n    kl_location = \\\"absent\\\"\\nelse:\\n    kl_location = \\\"ambiguous\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- “KL regularization as usual” earns at most **2/10**.\\n- Reporting only `beta=0.04` without saying where KL is applied earns at most **5/10**.\\n- A closed backend with hidden reference/KL internals cannot receive full credit.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Item 3 — Sampler, backend, and precision\\n\\nThe paper highlights sampler/backend/precision because the sampler defines the rollout distribution, and backend differences can move GRPO trajectories under the same visible hyperparameters. fileciteturn1file12\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Rollout engine and version | 2 |\\n| Decoding parameters: temperature, top-p/top-k, max tokens | 2 |\\n| Sampler precision and trainer precision | 2 |\\n| Tokenizer ID/revision and chat template | 2 |\\n| Same tokenizer/template in sampler and trainer, yes/no | 1 |\\n| Managed/backend-specific details or API version | 1 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\nvLLM, SGLang, generate, rollout engine,\\nOpenAI API, managed API, inference backend,\\ntemperature, top_p, top-k, max_tokens, max_new_tokens,\\nbf16, fp16, fp32, logits precision,\\ntokenizer, chat template, apply_chat_template,\\nrevision, commit hash\\n```\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nInspect:\\n\\n```text\\ngeneration_config.json\\ntokenizer_config.json\\nchat_template.jinja\\nconfigs/rollout.yaml\\nvllm_engine_args\\nsglang_args\\ntransformers.GenerationConfig\\ntemperature, top_p, top_k, do_sample\\ndtype, torch_dtype, bf16, fp16, fp32\\n```\\n\\n**Consistency checks**\\n\\n- Does the paper say `temp=0.8` but config says `temperature: 1.0`?\\n- Does the sampler use vLLM while the trainer uses Hugging Face `generate`?\\n- Is the tokenizer loaded separately in sampler and trainer?\\n- Does the repo pin model revision or only a floating model name?\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- “Generated with default settings” earns at most **2/10**.\\n- Naming vLLM without decoding parameters earns at most **4/10**.\\n- Naming precision only for training, not rollout sampling, earns at most **6/10**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Item 4 — Per-step ZVF and GU trajectory\\n\\nThe checker treats this as an artifact-heavy item. The paper defines ZVF as the fraction of prompts whose group completions all get identical reward and GU as `1 − ZVF`; it emphasizes that this trajectory reveals whether the run had usable group-relative signal. fileciteturn1file12\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| ZVF/GU definition or accepted substitute | 2 |\\n| Per-step trajectory artifact released | 3 |\\n| Summary stats, e.g. ZVF@25, GU@25 | 1.5 |\\n| Collapse rule or equivalent triage | 1 |\\n| Coverage for every arm and seed | 1.5 |\\n| Schema is machine-readable | 1 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\nZVF, zero-variance fraction,\\nGU, gradient utilization,\\nusable signal, reward variance,\\nper-step reward variance, gradient-norm variance,\\ncollapse, first-five-step rule,\\nZVF >= 80%, reward <= 5%\\n```\\n\\n**Repo/log heuristics**\\n\\nLook for logs with columns:\\n\\n```text\\nstep\\nprompt_id\\ngroup_id\\ncompletion_id\\nreward\\nzvf\\ngu\\nreward_variance\\ngradient_norm_variance\\n```\\n\\nAcceptable artifact names:\\n\\n```text\\nzvf_gu.csv\\ntelemetry.jsonl\\ntrain_metrics.csv\\nwandb-history.jsonl\\ntensorboard event files\\n```\\n\\n**Derived-metric computation**\\n\\nIf raw group rewards are available, the auditor computes ZVF itself:\\n\\n```python\\ndef compute_zvf(group_rewards_by_prompt):\\n    zero_var = 0\\n    total = 0\\n    for prompt_id, rewards in group_rewards_by_prompt.items():\\n        total += 1\\n        if max(rewards) == min(rewards):\\n            zero_var += 1\\n    zvf = zero_var / total\\n    gu = 1.0 - zvf\\n    return zvf, gu\\n```\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- Reporting only final reward earns **0/10** for item 4.\\n- Reporting reward variance without prompt/group IDs earns at most **4/10**.\\n- A single plotted curve with no data artifact earns at most **6/10**.\\n- ZVF on one seed only, when multiple seeds are claimed, caps at **7/10**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Item 5 — Group-size schedule\\n\\nThe paper treats group size `G` as a major lever because it affects the probability of mixed-reward, gradient-usable groups. fileciteturn1file8\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Fixed or adaptive schedule stated | 2 |\\n| `G` value at every step, or machine-readable schedule | 3 |\\n| Adaptive rule, bounds, and window if adaptive | 2 |\\n| Compute accounting: rollouts/tokens/prompts | 1.5 |\\n| Baseline and variant comparability | 1.5 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\ngroup size, num generations, G=,\\nrollouts per prompt, samples per prompt,\\nadaptive group size, dynamic sampling,\\nAERO, double, halve, rolling ZVF,\\nbounds, window, schedule\\n```\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nLook for:\\n\\n```text\\nnum_generations\\nnum_return_sequences\\ngroup_size\\nrollouts_per_prompt\\nn_samples_per_prompt\\nadaptive_g\\nmin_group_size\\nmax_group_size\\nzvf_window\\n```\\n\\n**Schedule reconstruction**\\n\\n- If raw logs include prompt IDs and completions, infer `G` per prompt per step.\\n- If adaptive, verify that schedule changes match the stated rule.\\n- If a variant uses dynamic sampling, check whether compute/token budget is normalized against the baseline.\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- “Group size 8” earns full credit only if no adaptive code/log behavior contradicts it.\\n- If the method adaptively filters or resamples groups but reports only nominal `G`, cap at **5/10**.\\n- If DAPO-style dynamic sampling is present but no compute accounting is given, cap at **7/10**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Item 6 — Held-out split distinct from reward environment\\n\\nThe paper distinguishes online training reward from capability evidence and requires a held-out slice disjoint from training prompts, scored by a harness, with sample size and confidence interval. fileciteturn1file8\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Dataset/split name, sample size, seed | 2 |\\n| Explicit train/eval disjointness | 2 |\\n| Evaluation harness and scoring parser | 2 |\\n| Training reward reported separately from held-out accuracy | 1.5 |\\n| Confidence interval or statistical test | 1.5 |\\n| Checkpoint selection rule | 1 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\nheld-out, test set, validation set, disjoint,\\nGSM8K test, MATH test, benchmark split,\\nevaluation harness, lm-eval, grader, parser,\\nconfidence interval, CI, bootstrap, Wilson,\\npaired test, p-value, seed,\\ncheckpoint selection, best checkpoint, last checkpoint\\n```\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nInspect:\\n\\n```text\\neval.py\\nevaluate.py\\nlm_eval configs\\ndataset split loading\\ntrain.jsonl / test.jsonl\\nheldout_ids.txt\\ncheckpoint selection scripts\\n```\\n\\n**Disjointness verifier**\\n\\nIf datasets are included:\\n\\n- Hash normalized prompts.\\n- Compare train and eval IDs.\\n- Run n-gram overlap as a weaker fallback.\\n- Flag if eval prompts appear in reward-training files.\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- “Accuracy 94%” with no split earns at most **2/10**.\\n- Reporting training-set reward as “accuracy” earns **0–2/10**.\\n- Held-out score without N/CI caps at **7/10**.\\n- Selecting the best checkpoint by training reward without a stated rule triggers a warning.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Item 7 — Decontamination probe results\\n\\nThe paper requires both train/test contamination checks and parser behavior on adversarial format-only inputs, because reward hacking can come from overlap, parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, or train-prompt overfitting. fileciteturn1file5\\n\\n**What must be reported**\\n\\n| Subfield | Points |\\n|---|---:|\\n| Train/test overlap method: n-gram, MinHash, embedding, etc. | 2 |\\n| Quantitative overlap result | 2 |\\n| Threshold or decision rule | 1 |\\n| Parser adversarial format-only tests | 2 |\\n| Parser reject/accept rates | 1.5 |\\n| Scripts or fixtures released | 1 |\\n| Remediation for contaminated cases | 0.5 |\\n\\n**Paper heuristics**\\n\\nSearch for:\\n\\n```text\\ndecontamination, contamination, overlap,\\nn-gram, 8-gram, MinHash, embedding similarity,\\ntrain/test leakage, duplicate prompts,\\nparser adversarial, format-only, no-answer,\\nreward hacking, shortcut, parser rejects,\\nanswer extractor, format token\\n```\\n\\n**Repo heuristics**\\n\\nLook for:\\n\\n```text\\ndecontam.py\\noverlap.py\\nngram_overlap\\nminhash\\nfaiss\\nsentence-transformers\\nparser_tests.py\\ntest_reward_parser.py\\nformat_only_cases.jsonl\\nadversarial_parser_inputs.jsonl\\n```\\n\\n**Parser probe checks**\\n\\nThe auditor generates or loads cases like:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"input\\\": \\\"####\\\",\\n  \\\"model_output\\\": \\\"Therefore the answer is \\\\\\\\boxed{}\\\",\\n  \\\"expected_reward\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"reason\\\": \\\"format-only, no answer\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen it runs the reward parser if available and flags cases where format-only outputs receive positive reward.\\n\\n**False positives**\\n\\n- “We use the official test set” is not a decontamination probe.\\n- “No contamination was found” without method/result earns at most **3/10**.\\n- Overlap check without parser adversarial tests caps at **6/10**.\\n- Parser tests without train/test overlap caps at **6/10**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 4. Canonical output schema\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"tool\\\": \\\"MIN-REPORT-RL Auditor\\\",\\n  \\\"version\\\": \\\"0.1\\\",\\n  \\\"target\\\": {\\n    \\\"paper\\\": \\\"paper.pdf\\\",\\n    \\\"repo\\\": \\\"https://github.com/example/grpo-release\\\",\\n    \\\"commit\\\": \\\"7fd31c2\\\",\\n    \\\"method_labels_detected\\\": [\\\"GRPO\\\", \\\"DAPO\\\"]\\n  },\\n  \\\"overall\\\": {\\n    \\\"raw\\\": 58.5,\\n    \\\"max\\\": 70,\\n    \\\"normalized\\\": 84,\\n    \\\"badge\\\": \\\"Usable with caveats\\\",\\n    \\\"confidence\\\": 0.81\\n  },\\n  \\\"items\\\": {\\n    \\\"1_loss_form\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 8,\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": 0.86,\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"mostly_complete\\\",\\n      \\\"missing\\\": [\\\"baseline advantage normalization formula\\\"],\\n      \\\"warnings\\\": []\\n    },\\n    \\\"2_reference_kl\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 9,\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": 0.91,\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"mostly_complete\\\",\\n      \\\"missing\\\": [],\\n      \\\"warnings\\\": [\\\"paper says reverse KL; code variable name says approx_kl, verified as reverse estimator\\\"]\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"blocking_issues\\\": [\\n    \\\"No released adversarial parser fixtures; item 7 capped at 8.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"reviewer_summary\\\": \\\"The stack is mostly auditable. The main missing pieces are parser adversarial fixtures and the exact baseline advantage-normalization formula.\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 5. Example output A — strong paper plus repo\\n\\n## CLI summary\\n\\n```text\\nMIN-REPORT-RL Auditor v0.1\\nTarget: dapo_math_release\\nMode: strict\\n\\nOverall: 93/100\\nRaw: 65.0 / 70\\nBadge: COMPLETE\\nConfidence: 0.89\\n\\n[1] Loss form                         9.0/10  PASS\\n[2] Reference policy + KL handling    9.0/10  PASS\\n[3] Sampler / backend / precision     10.0/10 PASS\\n[4] Per-step ZVF / GU trajectory      9.0/10  PASS\\n[5] Group-size schedule               9.0/10  PASS\\n[6] Held-out split                    10.0/10 PASS\\n[7] Decontamination probes            9.0/10  PASS\\n\\nMain caveat:\\n- Item 4: ZVF/GU trajectory is released for all seeds, but only mean ZVF@25 is summarized in the paper; GU@25 is present in the CSV.\\n```\\n\\n## Reviewer-facing summary\\n\\n```md\\n# MIN-REPORT-RL Review\\n\\nVerdict: COMPLETE, 93/100.\\n\\nThe paper is reproducibility-auditable. It reports the GRPO-family stack with enough detail\\nto distinguish the algorithmic delta from the stack: loss ratio/clipping/masking/advantage\\nnormalization, frozen reference and KL placement, vLLM rollout configuration, sampler/trainer\\nprecision, ZVF/GU telemetry, group-size schedule, held-out disjoint evaluation, and decontamination.\\n\\nRemaining minor issue:\\n- Add GU@25 to the paper table instead of requiring readers to open telemetry.csv.\\n```\\n\\n## JSON excerpt\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"overall\\\": {\\n    \\\"normalized\\\": 93,\\n    \\\"badge\\\": \\\"Complete\\\",\\n    \\\"confidence\\\": 0.89\\n  },\\n  \\\"items\\\": {\\n    \\\"1_loss_form\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 9,\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"source\\\": \\\"paper\\\",\\n          \\\"location\\\": \\\"Appendix A.1\\\",\\n          \\\"text\\\": \\\"PPO ratio with asymmetric clip_low=0.2, clip_high=0.28; completion-only mask; per-group advantage normalization.\\\"\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"source\\\": \\\"repo\\\",\\n          \\\"path\\\": \\\"configs/dapo_math.yaml\\\",\\n          \\\"keys\\\": {\\n            \\\"loss.use_ratio\\\": true,\\n            \\\"loss.clip_low\\\": 0.2,\\n            \\\"loss.clip_high\\\": 0.28,\\n            \\\"loss.mask\\\": \\\"completion_only\\\",\\n            \\\"loss.adv_norm\\\": \\\"per_group\\\"\\n          }\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \\\"missing\\\": []\\n    },\\n    \\\"4_zvf_gu\\\": {\\n      \\\"score\\\": 9,\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"source\\\": \\\"logs\\\",\\n          \\\"path\\\": \\\"artifacts/telemetry/zvf_gu_seed*.csv\\\",\\n          \\\"columns\\\": [\\\"step\\\", \\\"seed\\\", \\\"zvf\\\", \\\"gu\\\", \\\"mean_reward\\\"]\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \\\"derived\\\": {\\n        \\\"mean_zvf_at_25\\\": 0.43,\\n        \\\"mean_gu_at_25\\\": 0.57,\\n        \\\"collapse_rule_trips\\\": \\\"0/8 seeds\\\"\\n      },\\n      \\\"missing\\\": [\\\"GU@25 not printed in paper table\\\"]\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 6. Example output B — typical “standard GRPO” paper, no repo\\n\\n```text\\nMIN-REPORT-RL Auditor v0.1\\nTarget: grpo_reasoning_paper.pdf\\nMode: paper-only\\n\\nOverall: 31/100\\nRaw: 21.5 / 70\\nBadge: NOT REPRODUCIBILITY-AUDITABLE\\nConfidence: 0.42\\n\\n[1] Loss form                         2.0/10  FAIL\\n    Evidence: \\\"We use the standard GRPO objective.\\\"\\n    Missing: ratio yes/no, clip bounds, token mask, advantage norm.\\n\\n[2] Reference policy + KL handling    2.5/10  FAIL\\n    Evidence: \\\"KL regularization is applied.\\\"\\n    Missing: frozen reference, KL location, beta schedule, estimator.\\n\\n[3] Sampler / backend / precision     4.0/10  PARTIAL\\n    Evidence: \\\"We sample 8 responses at temperature 0.7.\\\"\\n    Missing: rollout engine, top_p, max_tokens, precision, tokenizer/chat-template identity.\\n\\n[4] Per-step ZVF / GU trajectory      0.0/10  FAIL\\n    Missing: no ZVF, GU, reward variance, or group-signal trajectory.\\n\\n[5] Group-size schedule               4.0/10  PARTIAL\\n    Evidence: \\\"G=8.\\\"\\n    Missing: per-step schedule; no confirmation fixed vs adaptive.\\n\\n[6] Held-out split                    5.0/10  PARTIAL\\n    Evidence: \\\"GSM8K test accuracy is reported.\\\"\\n    Missing: disjointness statement, CI, checkpoint selection rule.\\n\\n[7] Decontamination probes            4.0/10  PARTIAL\\n    Evidence: \\\"We remove duplicate train/test examples.\\\"\\n    Missing: overlap metric, quantitative result, parser adversarial tests.\\n\\nBlocking reviewer note:\\nThis paper should not be treated as an auditable GRPO comparison. The algorithm label is\\nreported, but most stack fields that MIN-REPORT-RL treats as flip levers are absent.\\n```\\n\\n## Suggested author fixes\\n\\n```md\\nRequired before reproducibility review:\\n1. Add a MIN-REPORT-RL block to the appendix.\\n2. State whether the loss uses an old-policy ratio, clipping, completion-only masking, and per-group normalization.\\n3. State whether KL is in the loss, reward, or absent.\\n4. Release per-step group-reward telemetry or ZVF/GU CSV.\\n5. Report held-out N, CI, disjointness, and checkpoint selection.\\n6. Add train/test overlap and reward-parser adversarial probes.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7. Example output C — repo found, paper underspecified\\n\\n```text\\nMIN-REPORT-RL Auditor v0.1\\nTarget: mad-grpo-code-release\\nMode: paper+repo\\n\\nOverall: 76/100\\nRaw: 53.0 / 70\\nBadge: USABLE WITH CAVEATS\\nConfidence: 0.78\\n\\nThe paper itself is incomplete, but the repo makes the stack partially reconstructable.\\n\\nItem scores:\\n[1] Loss form                         8/10\\n[2] Reference policy + KL handling    7/10\\n[3] Sampler / backend / precision     8/10\\n[4] Per-step ZVF / GU trajectory      5/10\\n[5] Group-size schedule               9/10\\n[6] Held-out split                    8/10\\n[7] Decontamination probes            8/10\\n\\nContradictions:\\n- Paper says \\\"completion-only loss\\\"; repo default is `loss_mask: full_sequence` in `configs/base.yaml`.\\n- Experiment override `configs/mad_grpo_math.yaml` changes this to `completion_only`, so the run is probably compliant, but the paper should cite the exact config.\\n\\nCaps applied:\\n- Item 4 capped at 5 because only aggregate ZVF@25 is reported; no per-step trajectory artifact is released.\\n```\\n\\n## JSON excerpt\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"overall\\\": {\\n    \\\"normalized\\\": 76,\\n    \\\"badge\\\": \\\"Usable with caveats\\\",\\n    \\\"confidence\\\": 0.78\\n  },\\n  \\\"contradictions\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"field\\\": \\\"loss_form.token_mask\\\",\\n      \\\"paper\\\": \\\"completion-only\\\",\\n      \\\"repo_default\\\": \\\"full_sequence\\\",\\n      \\\"experiment_override\\\": \\\"completion_only\\\",\\n      \\\"resolution\\\": \\\"experiment override wins, but paper should cite config path\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"caps\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"item\\\": \\\"4_zvf_gu\\\",\\n      \\\"cap\\\": 5,\\n      \\\"reason\\\": \\\"No released per-step trajectory.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 8. Example output D — closed managed backend\\n\\n```text\\nMIN-REPORT-RL Auditor v0.1\\nTarget: managed_api_grpo_study\\nMode: strict\\n\\nOverall: 49/100\\nRaw: 34.5 / 70\\nBadge: NOT REPRODUCIBILITY-AUDITABLE\\nConfidence: 0.55\\n\\nImportant scoping warning:\\nThe work uses a closed managed RL backend. The authors disclose this, but the loss form and\\nreference/KL implementation are not inspectable. The result should be scoped as:\\n\\\"ManagedBackend-X GRPO implementation\\\"\\nnot:\\n\\\"GRPO generally.\\\"\\n\\nScores:\\n[1] Loss form                         4/10  capped: closed backend\\n[2] Reference policy + KL handling    4/10  capped: closed backend\\n[3] Sampler / backend / precision     7/10\\n[4] Per-step ZVF / GU trajectory      0/10\\n[5] Group-size schedule               7/10\\n[6] Held-out split                    8/10\\n[7] Decontamination probes            4.5/10\\n\\nReviewer note:\\nThis may still be a useful platform result, but it is not enough evidence for an algorithmic\\nGRPO-family claim unless the hidden stack fields are disclosed or the claim is explicitly scoped.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 9. Anti-gaming rules\\n\\nThe checker should be conservative because the paper’s whole point is that visible hyperparameters are not enough.\\n\\n## Ambiguous prose penalties\\n\\n| Phrase | Maximum credit |\\n|---|---:|\\n| “standard GRPO loss” | 2/10 on item 1 |\\n| “KL as usual” | 2/10 on item 2 |\\n| “default generation settings” | 2/10 on item 3 |\\n| “we report reward curves” | 3/10 on item 4 unless ZVF/GU or substitute is present |\\n| “G=8” with adaptive code present | 5/10 on item 5 |\\n| “evaluated on GSM8K” | 4/10 on item 6 unless split/disjointness/N/CI are present |\\n| “no contamination” | 3/10 on item 7 unless method and result are reported |\\n\\n## Contradiction handling\\n\\nWhen paper and repo disagree:\\n\\n```text\\nitem_score = min(paper_score, repo_score_after_resolution)\\nwarning = \\\"paper/repo contradiction\\\"\\n```\\n\\nIf a launch config overrides a default, the checker accepts the launch config only if it can identify the exact experiment command, config merge order, or run artifact.\\n\\n## Default handling\\n\\nFramework defaults count only when all are true:\\n\\n1. framework and version are pinned;\\n2. the exact trainer class is identified;\\n3. the repo does not override the default;\\n4. the default is documented or visible in code.\\n\\nOtherwise defaults are treated as uncertain.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 10. Recommended canonical artifact\\n\\nThe easiest way for papers to pass the checker is to ship this file:\\n\\n```text\\nartifacts/min_report_rl.json\\n```\\n\\nMinimal schema:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"method_label\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n  \\\"base_checkpoint\\\": {\\n    \\\"id\\\": \\\"Qwen/Qwen3-8B\\\",\\n    \\\"revision\\\": \\\"...\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"tokenizer_chat_template\\\": {\\n    \\\"tokenizer_id\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n    \\\"chat_template_hash\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n    \\\"same_sampler_trainer\\\": true\\n  },\\n  \\\"loss_form\\\": {\\n    \\\"ppo_ratio_used\\\": true,\\n    \\\"clip\\\": {\\\"type\\\": \\\"symmetric\\\", \\\"epsilon\\\": 0.2},\\n    \\\"token_mask\\\": \\\"completion_only\\\",\\n    \\\"advantage_norm\\\": {\\n      \\\"scope\\\": \\\"per_group\\\",\\n      \\\"formula\\\": \\\"(r - mean_group) / std_group\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"reference_kl\\\": {\\n    \\\"frozen_reference\\\": true,\\n    \\\"reference_checkpoint\\\": \\\"SFT checkpoint hash\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_location\\\": \\\"loss\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_coeff\\\": 0.04,\\n    \\\"kl_schedule\\\": \\\"constant\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_estimator\\\": \\\"reverse_per_token\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"sampler_backend_precision\\\": {\\n    \\\"rollout_engine\\\": \\\"vLLM\\\",\\n    \\\"rollout_engine_version\\\": \\\"0.x\\\",\\n    \\\"decoding\\\": {\\\"temperature\\\": 0.8, \\\"top_p\\\": 1.0, \\\"max_tokens\\\": 512},\\n    \\\"precision\\\": {\\\"sampler_logits\\\": \\\"bf16\\\", \\\"trainer_logits\\\": \\\"bf16\\\"}\\n  },\\n  \\\"zvf_gu_trajectory\\\": {\\n    \\\"artifact\\\": \\\"telemetry/zvf_gu.csv\\\",\\n    \\\"per_step_logged\\\": true,\\n    \\\"mean_zvf_at_25\\\": 0.43,\\n    \\\"mean_gu_at_25\\\": 0.57,\\n    \\\"collapse_rule_trips\\\": \\\"0/8\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"group_size_schedule\\\": {\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"fixed\\\",\\n    \\\"G\\\": 8,\\n    \\\"artifact\\\": \\\"telemetry/group_size_by_step.csv\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"heldout_split\\\": {\\n    \\\"dataset\\\": \\\"GSM8K\\\",\\n    \\\"split\\\": \\\"test\\\",\\n    \\\"N\\\": 500,\\n    \\\"seed\\\": 0,\\n    \\\"disjoint_from_train\\\": true,\\n    \\\"harness\\\": \\\"eval/evaluate_gsm8k.py\\\",\\n    \\\"base_score\\\": 0.82,\\n    \\\"post_score\\\": 0.833,\\n    \\\"ci\\\": \\\"Wilson 95%\\\",\\n    \\\"reported_separately_from_training_reward\\\": true\\n  },\\n  \\\"decontamination\\\": {\\n    \\\"overlap_metric\\\": \\\"8-gram overlap\\\",\\n    \\\"overlap_value\\\": 0.0,\\n    \\\"parser_adversarial_artifact\\\": \\\"tests/format_only_cases.jsonl\\\",\\n    \\\"format_only_reject_rate\\\": 1.0\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis matches the paper’s adoption idea: trainers should emit the seven-field block and per-step telemetry by default, because the compliance cost should be close to a JSON block plus logged trajectories. fileciteturn1file1\"}]},{\"id\":\"6a37a9b3-a2b0-83e8-8e59-f5addc5ddbcd\",\"title\":\"Claim Extraction Bot\",\"current_node\":\"d5ef5533-1388-46f9-8632-19c065a6b44f\",\"n_nodes\":13,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"ct\":\"text\",\"len\":44760,\"t\":\"Read the attached paper. Invent a bot that scans GRPO-family papers, extracts every claim tied to a specific stack lever, and checks whether that lever is actually reported in the cited paper. Explain the claim-extraction model and the report format.\\n\\n### File: zvf-program/position/min_report_rl.tex\\nLines: 1-692\\n```\\n  1 | % =============================================================================\\n  2 | % MIN-REPORT-RL: A Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard for\\n  3 | %                Reinforcement-Learning Post-Training of LLMs\\n  4 | % =============================================================================\\n  5 | % Pillar 4 of the ZVF Program. POSITION PAPER (NeurIPS/ICML Position Track +\\n  6 | % MLRC / ICLR Reproducibility Track).\\n  7 | %\\n  8 | % This is a standalone draft. It is self-contained and should compile with a\\n  9 | % plain `article` class (no external .sty or .bib required: citations are\\n 10 | % rendered as inline TODO keys via a redefined \\\\cite, and the bibliography is\\n 11 | % a manual thebibliography stub). When promoting to a venue template, swap the\\n 12 | % preamble for neurips_2026.sty + \\\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib} and replace the\\n 13 | % TODO \\\\cite keys / thebibliography stub with a real .bib.\\n 14 | % =============================================================================\\n 15 | \\n 16 | \\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n 17 | \\n 18 | \\\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\\n 19 | \\\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\\n 20 | \\\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\\n 21 | \\\\usepackage{hyperref}\\n 22 | \\\\usepackage{url}\\n 23 | \\\\usepackage{booktabs}\\n 24 | \\\\usepackage{amsmath}\\n 25 | \\\\usepackage{amssymb}\\n 26 | \\\\usepackage{xcolor}\\n 27 | \\\\usepackage{enumitem}\\n 28 | \\\\usepackage{microtype}\\n 29 | \\\\usepackage{graphicx} % for \\\\resizebox on wide tables\\n 30 | \\n 31 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 32 | % TODO-citation shim. We do NOT invent bib entries with fake authors/years.\\n 33 | % Every \\\\cite below uses a clearly-TODO key and renders in red so a human can\\n 34 | % find and replace it with a real reference + .bib entry before submission.\\n 35 | % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n 36 | \\\\renewcommand{\\\\cite}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{[\\\\textsc{cite:}\\\\,#1]}}\\n 37 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\todo}[1]{\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO:\\\\ #1]}}}\\n 38 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\zvf}{\\\\textsc{Zvf}}\\n 39 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\gu}{\\\\textsc{Gu}}\\n 40 | \\\\newcommand{\\\\minreport}{\\\\textsc{Min-Report-RL}}\\n 41 | \\n 42 | \\\\title{\\\\minreport{}: Reporting the Stack, Not the Label\\\\\\\\\\n 43 | \\\\large A Community Minimum-Reportable-Stack Standard and\\n 44 | Reproducibility-Audit Protocol\\\\\\\\for Group-Relative RL Post-Training of LLMs}\\n 45 | \\n 46 | \\\\author{Arvind C R \\\\and the ZVF Program\\\\thanks{Pillar~4 of the ZVF Program.\\n 47 | Author list and affiliations \\\\todo{finalize author block and affiliations}.}}\\n 48 | \\n 49 | \\\\date{\\\\today}\\n 50 | \\n 51 | \\\\begin{document}\\n 52 | \\\\maketitle\\n 53 | \\n 54 | % =============================================================================\\n 55 | \\\\begin{abstract}\\n 56 | % =============================================================================\\n 57 | Reinforcement-learning post-training of large language models is reported as\\n 58 | though an algorithm label---PPO, GRPO, DPO, and the growing GRPO family\\n 59 | (DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, \\\\ldots)---fixes the experiment. It does not.\\n 60 | In a controlled audit of a group-relative RL runner, nominally identical\\n 61 | ``GRPO'' configurations (same model, group size, learning rate, dataset,\\n 62 | seed, and step budget) produced last-10 training reward of $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on one\\n 63 | backend and $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on another---a $\\\\sim 17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap with \\\\emph{no visible\\n 64 | hyperparameter difference}. The label was held constant; the \\\\emph{stack} was\\n 65 | not. We argue that this stack-conditioning is not an exotic edge case but the\\n 66 | default state of the field, and that the literature's current reporting norms\\n 67 | make it structurally impossible to tell whether a claimed algorithmic gain\\n 68 | survives a change of backend, sampler, reference-policy/KL handling, LoRA\\n 69 | configuration, or reward parser.\\n 70 | \\n 71 | This position paper proposes \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item\\n 72 | \\\\emph{minimum-reportable-stack} checklist that every GRPO-family paper should\\n 73 | satisfy, where each item is included precisely because it is a documented\\n 74 | lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison. We then propose a concrete\\n 75 | reproducibility-audit protocol---re-implement DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n 76 | MAD-GRPO inside \\\\emph{one} controlled stack and report which claimed gains\\n 77 | survive---and we specify the audit's experimental design, with results tables\\n 78 | whose cells are explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus. We\\n 79 | close with an adoption path for getting TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF to log the\\n 80 | \\\\minreport{} fields by default, and with responses to the obvious objections.\\n 81 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific GRPO variant is overstated; we claim\\n 82 | that, under current reporting, the field cannot \\\\emph{know}, and that this is\\n 83 | fixable with a few lines of telemetry.\\n 84 | \\\\end{abstract}\\n 85 | \\n 86 | % =============================================================================\\n 87 | \\\\section{Introduction: The Telemetry and Reporting Gap}\\n 88 | \\\\label{sec:intro}\\n 89 | % =============================================================================\\n 90 | \\n 91 | A reader of the 2023--2026 RL-for-LLM literature could be forgiven for\\n 92 | believing that ``we trained with GRPO'' is a complete experimental\\n 93 | description. It is not. The same three-letter label is attached to runs that\\n 94 | differ in their loss form (is there a PPO ratio? a clip? a completion-only\\n 95 | token mask?), their reference-policy and KL handling (frozen reference? KL\\n 96 | penalty in the loss, or in the reward, or absent?), their sampler and backend\\n 97 | (vLLM vs.\\\\ a managed inference API; bf16 vs.\\\\ fp32 logits), their group-size\\n 98 | schedule, and the parser that converts a generation into a scalar reward. Each\\n 99 | of these is, individually, sufficient to move a result. Together they form a\\n100 | \\\\emph{treatment} that the algorithm label does not name.\\n101 | \\n102 | This is the LLM-post-training analogue of the deep-RL reproducibility crisis\\n103 | documented a decade ago \\\\cite{henderson2018deeprl, islam2017reproducibility},\\n104 | now recurring one abstraction level up. The earlier crisis was about code-level\\n105 | and hyperparameter variance within a single algorithm; the present one is\\n106 | worse, because the ``stack'' spans a managed API whose loss we cannot inspect,\\n107 | a tokenizer and chat template that silently change the prompt, a sampler whose\\n108 | numerics differ from the trainer's, and a reward parser that can reward a\\n109 | format artifact instead of a correct answer.\\n110 | \\n111 | \\\\paragraph{The concrete trigger.}\\n112 | In an audit of a critic-free group-relative runner \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}, we\\n113 | attempted a deliberately boring comparison: hold the \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n114 | configuration fixed---Qwen3-8B, group size $G=8$, learning rate $10^{-5}$,\\n115 | GSM8K, 30 steps, seed~42---and swap only the backend. A managed runner reached\\n116 | $84.4\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} last-10 training reward; TRL on an H100 reached $5.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}. No visible\\n117 | hyperparameter explains the gap. What differs is everything the label omits:\\n118 | checkpoint and tokenizer identity, prompt construction, sampler behavior, loss\\n119 | masking, KL/reference handling, optimizer defaults, LoRA target modules,\\n120 | precision, rollout plumbing, checkpoint selection, and the evaluator. The\\n121 | comparison is not evidence that one backend is better; it is evidence that\\n122 | ``the GRPO config'' was \\\\emph{under-specified}. If a backend swap can move a\\n123 | result by $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} with no visible knob change, then a head-to-head between\\n124 | two GRPO \\\\emph{variants}---each implemented in its own stack---tells us almost\\n125 | nothing about the variants.\\n126 | \\n127 | \\\\paragraph{Why this is a reporting problem, not just a science problem.}\\n128 | The gap above is not a bug to be fixed by a better trainer; it is information\\n129 | that is routinely \\\\emph{not logged}. Most papers do not report whether the KL\\n130 | term was in the loss or the reward, whether the token mask was completion-only,\\n131 | what the sampler precision was, or what the per-step group-size schedule looked\\n132 | like. They also rarely report the per-step optimization telemetry---in our\\n133 | framework, the Zero-Variance Fraction (\\\\zvf{}) and Gradient Utilization\\n134 | (\\\\gu{})---that would reveal whether a run had any usable learning signal at all\\n135 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A field cannot audit what it does not record. The fix is\\n136 | cheap: a small, fixed set of fields, logged by default.\\n137 | \\n138 | \\\\paragraph{Scope and provenance of the worked numbers.}\\n139 | The concrete numbers in this paper used as motivating examples\\n140 | ($84.4\\\\%$/$\\\\,5.0\\\\%$/$\\\\,17\\\\times$ in the audit;\\n141 | $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$ prompt-token loss magnitude;\\n142 | $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$ held-out control at $p=0.26$;\\n143 | $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$/$\\\\,95.0\\\\%$ Llama-3.3-70B run)\\n144 | are \\\\emph{inherited from the v1 audit paper that motivated this\\n145 | position piece} \\\\todo{cite v1 audit paper, once assigned} and are\\n146 | marked inline with \\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} wherever they\\n147 | appear. A reader who wants to verify the numbers should consult that\\n148 | paper; this manuscript presents them as illustrative of the\\n149 | \\\\emph{kind} of stack-driven flip, not as re-derivable from a fresh\\n150 | run. Once the v1 audit paper is in citation scope, the inline\\n151 | \\\\todo{trace} markers are replaced with real \\\\texttt{\\\\textbackslash cite\\\\{zvfaudit\\\\}}\\n152 | keys and the corresponding bibliography entry below is filled in.\\n153 | We do \\\\emph{not} claim that any specific number here is independently\\n154 | verifiable against the present manuscript's experimental record.\\n155 | \\n156 | \\\\paragraph{Contributions.}\\n157 | This paper makes three:\\n158 | \\\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]\\n159 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{The stack-conditioning thesis, sharpened into a reporting\\n160 |         standard.} We restate and extend the audit finding that algorithm\\n161 |         labels are under-specified treatments (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}), and convert\\n162 |         it into \\\\minreport{}: a seven-item minimum-reportable-stack checklist\\n163 |         in which every item is justified by a mechanism through which it can\\n164 |         flip a comparison (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}).\\n165 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{A controlled reproducibility-audit protocol.} We specify a\\n166 |         single-stack re-implementation of DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO,\\n167 |         with a pre-registered analysis that reports which claimed gains survive\\n168 |         when the stack is held fixed (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}). Result cells are\\n169 |         explicit placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n170 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{An adoption path.} We describe concrete, low-friction changes\\n171 |         to TRL, verl, and OpenRLHF so that the \\\\minreport{} fields are emitted\\n172 |         by default, plus a venue checklist (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}).\\n173 | \\\\end{enumerate}\\n174 | \\n175 | % =============================================================================\\n176 | \\\\section{The Stack-Conditioning Problem}\\n177 | \\\\label{sec:stack}\\n178 | % =============================================================================\\n179 | \\n180 | \\\\paragraph{Thesis.}\\n181 | \\\\emph{Algorithm rankings in RL post-training are stack-conditioned.} A claim of\\n182 | the form ``method $A$ beats method $B$'' is, in current practice, a claim about\\n183 | two \\\\emph{full stacks} $S_A$ and $S_B$ that happen to be tagged $A$ and $B$. The\\n184 | quantities that determine the outcome---backend and sampler numerics, the exact\\n185 | loss form (ratio, clip, token mask), reference-policy and KL handling, LoRA\\n186 | target modules and rank, precision, the reward parser, the group-size schedule,\\n187 | checkpoint selection, and the evaluation harness---are co-determinants of the\\n188 | result, and they are typically \\\\emph{confounded with the label}. In a one-way\\n189 | variance decomposition over a heterogeneous run corpus, the framework/library\\n190 | factor and the training-algorithm factor each explain roughly half of the\\n191 | variance in the outcome ($\\\\eta^2 \\\\approx 0.55$ for both), but these factors are\\n192 | themselves confounded with model, backend, reward, hardware, and task\\n193 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. ``Algorithm'' and ``stack'' are not separable in the\\n194 | existing literature because nobody reports the stack.\\n195 | \\n196 | \\\\paragraph{A comparison that flips.}\\n197 | Consider the minimal worked example from the audit. The \\\\emph{visible} GRPO\\n198 | configuration is matched exactly:\\n199 | \\\\begin{center}\\n200 | \\\\small\\n201 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\\n202 | \\\\toprule\\n203 | \\\\textbf{Visible config (held constant)} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_1$} & \\\\textbf{Backend $S_2$}\\\\\\\\\\n204 | \\\\midrule\\n205 | Model & Qwen3-8B & Qwen3-8B \\\\\\\\\\n206 | Group size $G$ & 8 & 8 \\\\\\\\\\n207 | Learning rate & $10^{-5}$ & $10^{-5}$ \\\\\\\\\\n208 | Dataset / split & GSM8K-500 & GSM8K-500 \\\\\\\\\\n209 | Steps & 30 & 30 \\\\\\\\\\n210 | Seed & 42 & 42 \\\\\\\\\\n211 | \\\\midrule\\n212 | \\\\textbf{Last-10 training reward} & $\\\\mathbf{84.4\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} & $\\\\mathbf{5.0\\\\%}$~\\\\todo{trace} \\\\\\\\\\n213 | \\\\bottomrule\\n214 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n215 | \\\\end{center}\\n216 | A na\\\\\\\"ive reader concludes ``$S_1$'s GRPO is $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} better.'' The correct\\n217 | reading is that the two rows are \\\\emph{different treatments wearing the same\\n218 | label}: the loss form, token mask, KL/reference handling, sampler precision,\\n219 | LoRA targets, optimizer defaults, and reward parser were never held fixed\\n220 | because they were never reported, hence never matched. Flip any one of them and\\n221 | the ranking can invert. This is the entire problem in one table: the label is\\n222 | constant, the conclusion is determined by the unreported stack.\\n223 | \\n224 | \\\\paragraph{Why the GRPO family makes this acute.}\\n225 | The methods this paper targets---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, MAD-GRPO, and the broader\\n226 | variance-mitigation line (AERO, CPPO, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO)\\n227 | \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025, aero2024, cppo2024,\\n228 | ngrpo2025, scafgrpo2025}---are typically defined as \\\\emph{small deltas} on a\\n229 | base GRPO loop: a changed advantage normalization, a clip-bound tweak, a token\\n230 | mask, a length penalty, an exploration bonus, an adaptive group size. The size\\n231 | of the claimed improvement is frequently \\\\emph{smaller} than the stack effect\\n232 | demonstrated above. When the treatment effect you are trying to measure is\\n233 | $2$--$5$ points and the nuisance effect of an unreported stack difference is\\n234 | tens of points, an uncontrolled head-to-head is not measuring the method. The\\n235 | GRPO family is exactly the regime in which stack-conditioning is most likely to\\n236 | masquerade as algorithmic progress.\\n237 | \\n238 | % =============================================================================\\n239 | \\\\section{The \\\\minreport{} Standard}\\n240 | \\\\label{sec:standard}\\n241 | % =============================================================================\\n242 | \\n243 | \\\\minreport{} is a minimum-reportable-stack: the smallest set of fields such\\n244 | that, if two GRPO-family papers both report them, a reader can tell whether\\n245 | their comparison is confounded. Each item below is included \\\\emph{because there\\n246 | is a known mechanism by which it can flip a comparison}; an item that could not\\n247 | change a ranking would not earn its place on a minimum list.\\n248 | \\n249 | \\\\paragraph{1. Loss form.}\\n250 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether the update uses a PPO-style importance ratio\\n251 | $w_{i,t}=\\\\pi_\\\\theta/\\\\pi_{\\\\theta_{\\\\text{old}}}$; whether and how it is clipped\\n252 | (and the clip bounds, including asymmetric DAPO-style ``clip-higher'');\\n253 | whether the token mask is completion-only or whole-sequence; and whether\\n254 | advantages are normalized per-group, per-batch, or with a running estimate.\\n255 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the choice of token mask alone changes which tokens\\n256 | carry gradient. In one diagnostic, $61.6$--$89.6\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} of the full-sequence loss\\n257 | magnitude came from \\\\emph{prompt} tokens rather than completion tokens\\n258 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}; a whole-sequence mask and a completion-only mask are\\n259 | therefore different objectives sharing a name. Dr.GRPO's contribution is\\n260 | precisely a change to length/normalization in the loss \\\\cite{drgrpo2025}; GSPO\\n261 | changes the importance-sampling granularity \\\\cite{gspo2025}. If the baseline's\\n262 | loss form is unreported, the variant's gain is unattributable.\\n263 | \\n264 | \\\\paragraph{2. Reference policy and KL handling.}\\n265 | \\\\emph{Report:} whether a frozen reference policy is retained; whether the KL\\n266 | term sits in the \\\\emph{loss} or is folded into the \\\\emph{reward}; the KL\\n267 | coefficient and its schedule; and whether KL is estimated forward or reverse,\\n268 | per-token or per-sequence. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} KL placement changes the\\n269 | effective objective and the steady-state exploration. A runner that drops the\\n270 | reference policy entirely (one optimizer step per rollout, no frozen reference)\\n271 | is not doing the same thing as a KL-regularized GRPO, even at matched learning\\n272 | rate \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Methods that add a second anchor (e.g.\\\\ a dual-KL/SFT\\n273 | anchor) change steady-state \\\\zvf{} and must be recalibrated, not compared\\n274 | blind \\\\cite{dar2024}.\\n275 | \\n276 | \\\\paragraph{3. Sampler, backend, and precision.}\\n277 | \\\\emph{Report:} the rollout engine (vLLM, SGLang, a managed API, the trainer's\\n278 | own \\\\texttt{generate}; decoding parameters (temperature, top-$p$,\\n279 | \\\\texttt{max\\\\_tokens}; logit precision in the sampler vs.\\\\ the trainer (bf16 /\\n280 | fp16 / fp32); and whether sampler and trainer share the same tokenizer and chat\\n281 | template. \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} the sampler defines the rollout distribution\\n282 | that the group-relative update consumes. A managed, closed-source runner and an\\n283 | open vLLM path can yield order-of-magnitude different training-reward\\n284 | trajectories under identical visible configs---this is the mechanism behind the\\n285 | $17\\\\times$ gap of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack}. Sampler precision shifts the probability\\n286 | of mixed-reward groups, and hence the available gradient.\\n287 | \\n288 | \\\\paragraph{4. Per-step \\\\zvf{} and \\\\gu{} trajectory.}\\n289 | \\\\emph{Report:} the per-step Zero-Variance Fraction \\\\zvf{} (fraction of prompts\\n290 | whose $G$ completions all receive identical reward, contributing zero gradient)\\n291 | and Gradient Utilization $\\\\gu{}=1-\\\\zvf{}$, logged for every training step.\\n292 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the telemetry that reveals \\\\emph{whether\\n293 | a run had any learning signal at all}. The mixed-group probability\\n294 | $P(\\\\text{usable}) \\\\approx \\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_x\\\\!\\\\left[1-(1-p_x)^G-p_x^G\\\\right]$\\n295 | shows that a group-relative update only has signal when sampled groups contain\\n296 | reward diversity \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A method can ``win'' a comparison purely\\n297 | because its stack happened to produce lower \\\\zvf{} (more usable groups), not\\n298 | because its algorithmic idea is better. Without the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory, a\\n299 | collapsed run and a saturated run are indistinguishable from their final reward.\\n300 | A fixed first-five-step rule (\\\\zvf{}$\\\\,\\\\ge 80\\\\%$ with reward $\\\\le 5\\\\%$) is a\\n301 | cheap collapse triage; reporting the trajectory lets a reader apply it.\\n302 | \\n303 | \\\\paragraph{5. Group-size schedule (fixed or adaptive).}\\n304 | \\\\emph{Report:} the group size $G$ at every step, and the rule that changes it\\n305 | if it is adaptive (e.g.\\\\ AERO-style doubling/halving on a rolling \\\\zvf{}\\n306 | estimate). \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} $G$ directly controls the mixed-group\\n307 | probability above and is one of the strongest single knobs we measured---an\\n308 | ablation on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K moved last-10 reward across a wide band as $G$ went\\n309 | $2\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!4\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!8\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!16$ \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. An adaptive-$G$ method (AERO)\\n310 | compared against a fixed-$G$ baseline is partly being credited for spending\\n311 | more compute on hard prompts, an effect that must be separated from the\\n312 | algorithmic claim \\\\cite{aero2024}.\\n313 | \\n314 | \\\\paragraph{6. Held-out split distinct from the reward environment.}\\n315 | \\\\emph{Report:} a held-out evaluation slice that is \\\\emph{disjoint} from the\\n316 | training prompts and scored by a harness, with sample size and a confidence\\n317 | interval, reported \\\\emph{separately} from online training reward. \\\\emph{Why it\\n318 | can flip:} online training reward is dynamics evidence, not capability\\n319 | evidence. In the audit, a clean paired control improved only $82.0\\\\%\\\\to83.3\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation}\\n320 | ($p=0.26$)~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on held-out GSM8K despite training reward near saturation; and\\n321 | selecting checkpoints by training reward produced an $87$--$95\\\\%$ ``capability''\\n322 | band that is a selection artifact, not a causal lift \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. A\\n323 | variant that reports only training reward can show a large apparent gain that\\n324 | vanishes held-out. Four Llama-3.3-70B seeds ranged $95.0$--$98.1\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} on training\\n325 | last-10 yet all landed on $95.0\\\\%$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} held-out---last-10 variance was\\n326 | sampling noise, not generalization.\\n327 | \\n328 | \\\\paragraph{7. Decontamination probe results.}\\n329 | \\\\emph{Report:} the outcome of an explicit train/test contamination check\\n330 | (n-gram or embedding overlap between training prompts and the held-out /\\n331 | benchmark slice), and the parser's behavior on adversarial format-only inputs.\\n332 | \\\\emph{Why it can flip:} verifiable rewards do not eliminate reward hacking;\\n333 | parser artifacts, format shortcuts, length effects, and train-prompt overfitting\\n334 | can all inflate a reward without capability gain \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. If the\\n335 | benchmark slice overlaps the reward environment, a ``gain'' may be memorization;\\n336 | if the parser rewards a format token, a ``gain'' may be a shortcut. A\\n337 | contamination/parser probe is the minimum evidence that the reported number\\n338 | measures the thing it claims to measure.\\n339 | \\n340 | \\\\paragraph{Summary table.}\\n341 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:checklist} collects the standard. The companion\\n342 | \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} provides a fillable appendix template.\\n343 | \\n344 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n345 | \\\\centering\\n346 | \\\\caption{The \\\\minreport{} minimum-reportable-stack. Each item is on the list\\n347 | because it is a documented lever that can flip a head-to-head comparison.}\\n348 | \\\\label{tab:checklist}\\n349 | \\\\small\\n350 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}rp{0.30\\\\linewidth}p{0.50\\\\linewidth}@{}}\\n351 | \\\\toprule\\n352 | \\\\# & \\\\textbf{Field} & \\\\textbf{Flip mechanism (why it is mandatory)}\\\\\\\\\\n353 | \\\\midrule\\n354 | 1 & Loss form (ratio / clip / token mask / advantage norm.) & Token mask reassigns gradient across prompt vs.\\\\ completion tokens; clip and norm.\\\\ are the actual variant deltas.\\\\\\\\\\n355 | 2 & Reference policy + KL handling & KL placement (loss vs.\\\\ reward) and a frozen-vs-absent reference change the objective and exploration.\\\\\\\\\\n356 | 3 & Sampler / backend / precision & The sampler defines the rollout distribution; backend/precision drove the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} matched-config gap.\\\\\\\\\\n357 | 4 & Per-step \\\\zvf{} / \\\\gu{} trajectory & Reveals whether the run had usable learning signal; separates collapse from saturation.\\\\\\\\\\n358 | 5 & Group-size schedule (fixed / adaptive) & $G$ sets the mixed-group probability; adaptive-$G$ confounds compute with algorithm.\\\\\\\\\\n359 | 6 & Held-out split $\\\\neq$ reward environment & Training reward is not capability; selection-by-reward inflates apparent gains.\\\\\\\\\\n360 | 7 & Decontamination probe results & Verifiable rewards still admit parser/format/length/overlap hacking.\\\\\\\\\\n361 | \\\\bottomrule\\n362 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n363 | \\\\end{table}\\n364 | \\n365 | % =============================================================================\\n366 | \\\\section{Proposed Reproducibility Audit}\\n367 | \\\\label{sec:audit}\\n368 | % =============================================================================\\n369 | \\n370 | The \\\\minreport{} standard tells authors what to report. The audit tells the\\n371 | community what to \\\\emph{check}: \\\\emph{which claimed GRPO-family gains survive\\n372 | when the stack is held fixed?}\\n373 | \\n374 | \\\\paragraph{Design.}\\n375 | Re-implement four prominent GRPO variants---DAPO, GSPO, Dr.GRPO, and\\n376 | MAD-GRPO \\\\cite{dapo2025, gspo2025, drgrpo2025, madgrpo2025}---as\\n377 | \\\\emph{minimal configuration overrides on a single shared trainer}. Everything\\n378 | in the \\\\minreport{} list except the variant's defining delta is held identical\\n379 | across arms: the same base checkpoint and tokenizer, the same vLLM sampler and\\n380 | precision, the same reference-policy/KL handling, the same LoRA targets and\\n381 | rank, the same optimizer, the same reward parser, the same group-size schedule\\n382 | (where the variant does not itself change it), the same held-out slice, and the\\n383 | same evaluation harness and seed sweep. Only the named hook differs (loss form,\\n384 | advantage normalization, clip rule, masking, or group-sizing). This is the same\\n385 | methodology already validated for the variance-mitigation head-to-head, where\\n386 | AERO/CPPO/NGRPO/Scaf-GRPO were each implemented as one hook on a shared GRPO\\n387 | trainer with tokenizer, sampler, optimizer, LoRA adapters, evaluation harness,\\n388 | and seed sweep held identical \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n389 | \\n390 | \\\\paragraph{Two readings per variant.}\\n391 | For each variant we report (i) its result \\\\emph{as published} (its own stack,\\n392 | its own numbers) and (ii) its result \\\\emph{in the controlled stack}. The\\n393 | quantity of interest is the \\\\emph{survival} of the claimed gain: the\\n394 | controlled-stack delta relative to the shared GRPO baseline, with a confidence\\n395 | interval and a survival verdict (survives / shrinks / disappears / reverses).\\n396 | \\n397 | \\\\paragraph{Pre-registration.}\\n398 | The arm list, the shared stack specification, the held-out slice, the seed set,\\n399 | and the survival thresholds are fixed before any controlled run is scored, to\\n400 | avoid the selection-by-reward over-optimism the audit itself warns against\\n401 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Every arm reports the full \\\\minreport{} block.\\n402 | \\n403 | \\\\paragraph{Primary results table.}\\n404 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit} is the headline deliverable; all numeric cells are\\n405 | placeholders to be filled from the audit corpus.\\n406 | \\n407 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n408 | \\\\centering\\n409 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Controlled single-stack audit of GRPO-family variants.}\\n410 | Each variant is a minimal hook on one shared trainer (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}); the\\n411 | full \\\\minreport{} block is identical across arms except the variant's defining\\n412 | delta. ``Published $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain the original paper reports on its own\\n413 | stack; ``Controlled $\\\\Delta$'' is the gain in the shared stack vs.\\\\ the shared\\n414 | GRPO baseline; ``Survives?'' is the pre-registered verdict. All cells are\\n415 | \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n416 | \\\\label{tab:audit}\\n417 | \\\\small\\n418 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n419 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\\n420 | \\\\toprule\\n421 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Defining delta} & \\\\textbf{Published $\\\\Delta$} &\\n422 | \\\\textbf{Controlled last-10} & \\\\textbf{Controlled held-out} &\\n423 | \\\\textbf{Controlled $\\\\Delta$ (95\\\\% CI)} & \\\\textbf{Survives?}\\\\\\\\\\n424 | \\\\midrule\\n425 | GRPO (baseline) & --- & --- & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & $0$ (ref.) & --- \\\\\\\\\\n426 | DAPO     & clip-higher + dyn.\\\\ sampling & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n427 | GSPO     & sequence-level IS ratio      & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n428 | Dr.GRPO  & length/normalization fix     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n429 | MAD-GRPO & multi-agent / diversity term & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n430 | \\\\bottomrule\\n431 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n432 | \\\\end{table}\\n433 | \\n434 | \\\\paragraph{Telemetry table.}\\n435 | Table~\\\\ref{tab:audit_telemetry} reports the \\\\minreport{} item-4 telemetry for\\n436 | each arm, which is the mechanism by which a ``gain'' might be a stack effect\\n437 | rather than an algorithmic one (e.g.\\\\ a variant that simply lowers \\\\zvf{}).\\n438 | \\n439 | \\\\begin{table}[t]\\n440 | \\\\centering\\n441 | \\\\caption{\\\\textbf{Per-arm \\\\minreport{} telemetry.} \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} and collapse\\n442 | behavior under the shared stack, to attribute any controlled gain to algorithm\\n443 | vs.\\\\ usable-signal availability. All cells are \\\\todo{fill from audit corpus}.}\\n444 | \\\\label{tab:audit_telemetry}\\n445 | \\\\small\\n446 | \\\\resizebox{\\\\textwidth}{!}{%\\n447 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\\n448 | \\\\toprule\\n449 | \\\\textbf{Variant} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\zvf{} @ step 25} & \\\\textbf{Mean \\\\gu{} @ step 25} &\\n450 | \\\\textbf{Collapse rate (seeds)} & \\\\textbf{Time-to-collapse (median steps)} &\\n451 | \\\\textbf{Group-size schedule}\\\\\\\\\\n452 | \\\\midrule\\n453 | GRPO (baseline) & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & fixed $G$ \\\\\\\\\\n454 | DAPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n455 | GSPO     & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n456 | Dr.GRPO  & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n457 | MAD-GRPO & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} & \\\\todo{} \\\\\\\\\\n458 | \\\\bottomrule\\n459 | \\\\end{tabular}}\\n460 | \\\\end{table}\\n461 | \\n462 | \\\\paragraph{Statistical protocol.}\\n463 | Because the audit's independent unit is a seed (not a per-step reward, which is\\n464 | autocorrelated within a trace), inferential claims are made only across seeds.\\n465 | We report mean $\\\\pm$ standard error and bootstrap 95\\\\% CIs across the seed\\n466 | sweep, control the false-discovery rate with Benjamini--Hochberg over the\\n467 | pre-registered set of survival tests, and treat single-seed arms as descriptive\\n468 | only. This mirrors the statistical discipline of the source audit, which\\n469 | explicitly refuses to upgrade trace-level descriptive effect sizes into\\n470 | inferential evidence about distinct seeds, runs, or stacks \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}.\\n471 | \\\\todo{Fix the seed count $S$ and the survival threshold once compute is\\n472 | budgeted; record minimum detectable effect at the chosen $S$.}\\n473 | \\n474 | \\\\paragraph{Honest scoping.}\\n475 | The audit's deliverable is \\\\emph{not} ``method $X$ is fake.'' It is a survival\\n476 | map: for each variant, the fraction of its claimed gain that persists when the\\n477 | stack is the same. Some gains will survive (they are real algorithmic\\n478 | contributions); some will shrink (they were partly stack); some may disappear\\n479 | (they were stack). All three outcomes are informative, and reporting them is\\n480 | the contribution.\\n481 | \\n482 | % =============================================================================\\n483 | \\\\section{Adoption Path}\\n484 | \\\\label{sec:adoption}\\n485 | % =============================================================================\\n486 | \\n487 | A standard nobody can comply with cheaply will not be adopted. \\\\minreport{} is\\n488 | designed so that the marginal cost to an author is close to zero \\\\emph{if the\\n489 | trainers log it by default}.\\n490 | \\n491 | \\\\paragraph{Make the trainers emit it.}\\n492 | The seven fields are already computable inside every major open trainer:\\n493 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n494 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{TRL.} The \\\\texttt{GRPOTrainer} already exposes the loss form,\\n495 |         KL handling, and sampler config; \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is a few lines over the\\n496 |         per-prompt group rewards it already computes, logged each step. Propose\\n497 |         a \\\\texttt{report\\\\_min\\\\_report\\\\_rl=True} flag that writes the seven-field\\n498 |         block to the run config and the \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} trajectory to the logger.\\n499 |         \\\\todo{file TRL issue / PR; link.}\\n500 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{verl.} Its rollout/actor separation makes sampler, precision,\\n501 |         and group-size schedule first-class; expose them in the run manifest\\n502 |         and add a per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} metric. \\\\todo{file verl issue / PR;\\n503 |         link.}\\n504 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{OpenRLHF.} Add the same manifest block and per-step telemetry;\\n505 |         its reference-model and KL options map directly onto item~2.\\n506 |         \\\\todo{file OpenRLHF issue / PR; link.}\\n507 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n508 | A shared, copy-pasteable emitter (a small library that takes a trainer's config\\n509 | and per-step group rewards and writes the \\\\minreport{} block as JSON) lets all\\n510 | three converge on the same schema. \\\\todo{publish the emitter package + schema.}\\n511 | The companion \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff} tool described in \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:tooling}\\n512 | then consumes these manifests to flag confounded comparisons automatically.\\n513 | \\n514 | \\\\paragraph{Make the venues ask for it.}\\n515 | Add a \\\\minreport{} block to the reproducibility checklist for the RL-for-LLM\\n516 | track, analogous to the existing NeurIPS/ICML reproducibility checklists. The\\n517 | companion \\\\texttt{CHECKLIST.md} is written to be dropped verbatim into a paper's\\n518 | appendix.\\n519 | \\n520 | \\\\paragraph{Make it auditable post hoc.}\\n521 | For already-published work, the same JSON block can be reconstructed from\\n522 | release artifacts where they exist. The audit of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit} doubles as a\\n523 | demonstration: it re-derives the \\\\minreport{} block for four variants and shows\\n524 | the format is sufficient to detect confounded comparisons.\\n525 | \\n526 | % =============================================================================\\n527 | \\\\section{Objections and Responses}\\n528 | \\\\label{sec:objections}\\n529 | % =============================================================================\\n530 | \\n531 | \\\\paragraph{``This is just `report your hyperparameters.'\\\\,''}\\n532 | No. Hyperparameter tables already exist and the $17\\\\times$~\\\\todo{trace to v1 audit citation} gap occurred\\n533 | \\\\emph{despite} matched visible hyperparameters. \\\\minreport{} names the fields\\n534 | that hyperparameter tables systematically omit---loss form, KL placement,\\n535 | sampler precision, per-step \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{}, group-size schedule, held-out\\n536 | disjointness, contamination probe---precisely because each is a demonstrated\\n537 | flip lever, not a tuning knob.\\n538 | \\n539 | \\\\paragraph{``Seven items is too many / too few.''}\\n540 | Each item earns its place by a flip mechanism (\\\\S\\\\ref{sec:standard}); removing\\n541 | any one reopens a known confound. The list is a \\\\emph{minimum}, not a maximum:\\n542 | authors may report more. We deliberately stop at seven because that is the set\\n543 | that, in our audit, was sufficient to explain the comparisons that flipped.\\n544 | \\\\todo{If the audit surfaces an eighth recurring confound, extend the list and\\n545 | justify it the same way.}\\n546 | \\n547 | \\\\paragraph{``Re-implementations are unfaithful, so the audit is unfair.''}\\n548 | The audit reports \\\\emph{both} readings (published and controlled) and frames the\\n549 | result as survival, not refutation. An unfaithful re-implementation is itself a\\n550 | finding: if a variant's gain depends on an unstated stack detail that a careful\\n551 | re-implementer cannot reproduce from the paper, that is a reporting failure the\\n552 | standard is designed to surface. We will publish every arm's full \\\\minreport{}\\n553 | block so disagreements are about specific fields, not vibes.\\n554 | \\n555 | \\\\paragraph{``Closed/managed backends can't comply.''}\\n556 | Partly true, and that is the point: a closed runner cannot expose its loss form\\n557 | or KL handling, so results from it should be scoped as ``this platform's\\n558 | implementation,'' not ``GRPO'' \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. The standard makes that\\n559 | scoping explicit rather than letting a closed-stack number stand in for an\\n560 | algorithm.\\n561 | \\n562 | \\\\paragraph{``\\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is your own metric; why mandate it?''}\\n563 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is one cheap instantiation of a necessary quantity---\\\\emph{did the\\n564 | run have usable group-relative signal?}---and we accept substitutes. Under\\n565 | dense process rewards or scaffolded exploration, \\\\zvf{} degenerates and should\\n566 | be replaced by per-step reward variance or a gradient-norm-variance surrogate\\n567 | \\\\cite{zvfaudit2026}. Item~4 mandates \\\\emph{a usable-signal trajectory}, of which\\n568 | \\\\zvf{}/\\\\gu{} is the default; the point is that \\\\emph{some} such telemetry must be\\n569 | reported.\\n570 | \\n571 | % =============================================================================\\n572 | \\\\section{Companion Tooling: \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}}\\n573 | \\\\label{sec:tooling}\\n574 | % =============================================================================\\n575 | \\n576 | A standard is easier to adopt if reviewers can check it mechanically. We sketch\\n577 | a companion CLI, \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}, that consumes two \\\\minreport{}\\n578 | run manifests and reports whether their differences are large enough to flip a\\n579 | comparison. The tool is not a replacement for the audit of\\n580 | \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:audit}; it is an enforcement layer that tells a reviewer, in\\n581 | seconds, whether a head-to-head is stack-comparable.\\n582 | \\n583 | \\\\paragraph{Manifest contract.} Each run is a YAML/JSON block containing the\\n584 | seven \\\\minreport{} fields, plus run identity (trainer, version, seed, hardware),\\n585 | model and tokenizer hashes, LoRA configuration, optimizer settings, and results.\\n586 | The schema is the JSON block proposed in \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:adoption}.\\n587 | \\n588 | \\\\paragraph{Diff taxonomy.} Every field difference receives three labels:\\n589 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=2pt]\\n590 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{Lever.} One of eight lever groups: L0~run identity/provenance,\\n591 |     L1~loss form, L2~reference policy and KL handling, L3~sampler/backend/\\n592 |     precision, L4~usable-signal telemetry (\\\\zvf/\\\\gu{}), L5~group-size\\n593 |     schedule, L6~held-out evaluation and checkpoint selection, L7~decontamination\\n594 |     and parser robustness.\\n595 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{Diff kind.} EQUAL, COSMETIC, PROVENANCE, PARAMETRIC, SCHEDULE,\\n596 |     SEMANTIC\\\\_OBJECTIVE, DISTRIBUTIONAL, TELEMETRY, MISSING, OPAQUE, DERIVED, or\\n597 |     INVALID\\\\_TARGET.\\n598 |   \\\\item \\\\textbf{Role.} TREATMENT\\\\_DELTA if the user declares it as the\\n599 |     algorithmic delta being tested; NUISANCE\\\\_DELTA if it is an uncontrolled\\n600 |     stack difference; COVERAGE\\\\_GAP if the field is missing; INVALIDATOR if the\\n601 |     comparison target is not common.\\n602 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n603 | \\n604 | \\\\paragraph{Flip-risk classes.} The tool compares each nuisance difference to\\n605 | the reported comparison margin and classifies the overall risk as one of:\\n606 | \\\\begin{center}\\n607 | \\\\small\\n608 | \\\\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}\\n609 | \\\\toprule\\n610 | \\\\textbf{Class} & \\\\textbf{Meaning}\\\\\\\\\\n611 | \\\\midrule\\n612 | R0 \\\\texttt{same} & No meaningful difference.\\\\\\\\\\n613 | R1 \\\\texttt{cosmetic} & Version string or comment only.\\\\\\\\\\n614 | R2 \\\\texttt{small} & Estimated effect $<25\\\\%$ of the comparison margin.\\\\\\\\\\n615 | R3 \\\\texttt{material} & Effect $25$--$100\\\\%$ of the margin; can shrink a claim.\\\\\\\\\\n616 | R4 \\\\texttt{flip\\\\_capable} & Effect bound $\\\\\\text{\\\\textgreater=}$ margin; can flip the ranking.\\\\\\\\\\n617 | R5 \\\\texttt{invalidating} & The comparison target is not common.\\\\\\\\\\n618 | RU \\\\texttt{unknown} & Missing or opaque evidence; comparison unverifiable.\\\\\\\\\\n619 | \\\\bottomrule\\n620 | \\\\end{tabular}\\n621 | \\\\end{center}\\n622 | \\n623 | \\\\paragraph{Verdict and CI integration.} The tool emits a deterministic verdict\\n624 | (\\\\texttt{STACK\\\\_MATCHED}, \\\\texttt{STACK\\\\_MATERIAL}, \\\\texttt{STACK\\\\_CONFOUNDED},\\n625 | \\\\texttt{UNVERIFIABLE}, or \\\\texttt{INVALID\\\\_COMPARISON}) and an exit code, so a\\n626 | paper's reproducibility workflow can fail a comparison that is\\n627 | flip-capable. Example:\\n628 | \\\\begin{verbatim}\\n629 | grpo-stackdiff compare dapo.yaml grpo.yaml \\\\\\n630 |   --metric heldout.exact_match --claim-delta +0.024 \\\\\\n631 |   --fail-on flip-capable\\n632 | \\\\end{verbatim}\\n633 | \\n634 | \\\\paragraph{Role in the \\\\minreport{} ecosystem.} Authors emit the JSON block;\\n635 | venues ask for it; reviewers run \\\\texttt{grpo-stackdiff}. The cost to an author\\n636 | is still a few lines of telemetry; the cost to a reviewer is one command. The\\n637 | tool turns the abstract stack-conditioning thesis of \\\\S\\\\ref{sec:stack} into a\\n638 | concrete, reproducible check.\\n639 | \\n640 | % =============================================================================\\n641 | \\\\section{Conclusion}\\n642 | \\\\label{sec:conclusion}\\n643 | % =============================================================================\\n644 | \\n645 | The RL-for-LLM literature is in a position the deep-RL community has seen\\n646 | before: a three-letter label is doing the work of a full experimental\\n647 | specification, and the unreported remainder of the stack can move a result by\\n648 | more than the algorithmic effect anyone is trying to claim. We have argued that\\n649 | this stack-conditioning is the default, demonstrated it with a matched-config\\n650 | comparison that flips by $17\\\\times$, and proposed two coupled remedies: a\\n651 | seven-item minimum-reportable-stack (\\\\minreport{}), where every item is a\\n652 | documented flip lever, and a controlled single-stack audit of DAPO, GSPO,\\n653 | Dr.GRPO, and MAD-GRPO that reports which claimed gains survive. Neither remedy\\n654 | requires new science---only a few fields of telemetry and the discipline to log\\n655 | them. The cost is a JSON block; the payoff is a literature whose comparisons one\\n656 | can actually trust.\\n657 | \\n658 | % =============================================================================\\n659 | % Bibliography. MANUAL STUB ONLY. We do NOT invent authors/years. Every entry\\n660 | % is a TODO key to be replaced by a real citation before submission. When\\n661 | % promoting to a venue template, delete this block, use \\\\usepackage{natbib},\\n662 | % and point \\\\bibliography at a real .bib.\\n663 | %\\n664 | % SUBMISSION GATE: a reviewer of this manuscript will see a red\\n665 | % [cite:KEY] marker for every in-text citation and a red [TODO] marker\\n666 | % for every entry below. The paper will be treated as a draft, not as\\n667 | % a submission, until the bibliography is real. The 12 cited works are\\n668 | % all findable; if the position paper cannot stand on the citations it\\n669 | % has, the claims need to be cut, not the citations stubbed.\\n670 | % =============================================================================\\n671 | \\\\section*{References}\\n672 | \\\\small\\n673 | \\\\noindent\\\\textcolor{red}{\\\\textbf{[TODO: replace this stub with a real .bib.\\n674 | Every \\\\texttt{[cite:KEY]} marker above corresponds to one entry below. Do not\\n675 | ship with placeholder keys. Each entry below carries a [\\\\textsc{required}]\\n676 | tag indicating its status; all are required for submission.]}}\\n677 | \\\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em, itemsep=1pt, label={}]\\n678 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{zvfaudit2026} --- \\\\todo{the ZVF Program v1 audit paper (this group's own work: ``Reward Contrast, Not Algorithm Labels''); cite the arXiv/venue version once assigned.}\\n679 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{henderson2018deeprl} --- \\\\todo{Henderson et al., ``Deep Reinforcement Learning that Matters'' (reproducibility crisis in deep RL).}\\n680 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{islam2017reproducibility} --- \\\\todo{Islam et al., reproducibility of benchmarked deep RL (verify exact reference).}\\n681 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dapo2025} --- \\\\todo{DAPO paper (clip-higher + dynamic sampling GRPO variant); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n682 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{gspo2025} --- \\\\todo{GSPO paper (sequence-level importance sampling); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n683 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{drgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Dr.GRPO paper (length/normalization fix); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n684 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{madgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{MAD-GRPO paper (multi-agent/diversity GRPO); fill authors/venue/year.}\\n685 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{aero2024} --- \\\\todo{AERO (adaptive rollout sizing); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n686 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{cppo2024} --- \\\\todo{CPPO (clip-pruned PPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n687 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{ngrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{NGRPO (normalized GRPO); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n688 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{scafgrpo2025} --- \\\\todo{Scaf-GRPO (scaffolded exploration); from variance-mitigation bib fragment.}\\n689 |   \\\\item \\\\texttt{dar2024} --- \\\\todo{DAR (dual-alignment / dual-KL regularization); from extended-RW bib fragment.}\\n690 | \\\\end{itemize}\\n691 | \\n692 | \\\\end{document}\\n```\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"ct\":\"text\",\"len\":18383,\"t\":\"## Bot: **LeverTrace**\\n\\n**LeverTrace** is a citation-audit bot for GRPO-family papers. It reads a paper, finds every atomic claim that depends on a stack lever, follows the citation attached to that claim, and checks whether the cited paper actually reports the lever detail needed to support the claim.\\n\\nThe motivation comes directly from the attached Min-Report-RL paper: it argues that “GRPO” is not a complete experimental description, because runs can differ in loss form, KL/reference handling, sampler/backend/precision, group-size schedule, reward parser, held-out evaluation, and telemetry while still wearing the same algorithm label. The paper’s core point is that algorithm rankings are really rankings over full stacks, not labels alone. fileciteturn1file1 fileciteturn1file11\\n\\nLeverTrace is therefore an upstream companion to the paper’s proposed `grpo-stackdiff`: `grpo-stackdiff` compares manifests; LeverTrace builds the claim/evidence ledger that tells reviewers whether the cited papers contain enough information to build those manifests. The paper already defines a useful downstream taxonomy: lever, diff kind, role, flip-risk class, and verdict. LeverTrace reuses that vocabulary but applies it to claims and citations rather than only to two completed run manifests. fileciteturn1file0\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. What LeverTrace scans for\\n\\nLeverTrace extracts only **lever-bound claims**: claims whose truth, attribution, or interpretability depends on one of the Min-Report-RL stack fields.\\n\\nThe lever ontology is:\\n\\n| Lever | What LeverTrace looks for | Required reporting evidence |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **L0: Provenance** | trainer, version, seed, hardware, model/tokenizer hashes, optimizer, LoRA config | explicit run identity or linked artifact |\\n| **L1: Loss form** | PPO ratio, clipping, clip bounds, asymmetric clip-higher, token mask, advantage normalization, length normalization | formula, objective description, mask definition, normalization rule |\\n| **L2: Reference policy / KL** | frozen reference, absent reference, KL in loss vs reward, KL coefficient, schedule, forward/reverse KL, token/sequence KL | explicit KL/reference description |\\n| **L3: Sampler / backend / precision** | vLLM/SGLang/API/generate, temperature, top-p, max tokens, bf16/fp16/fp32, tokenizer/chat template sharing | sampler config and precision/tokenizer details |\\n| **L4: Usable-signal telemetry** | ZVF, GU, reward variance, gradient-signal proxy, collapse rule | per-step trajectory or substitute metric |\\n| **L5: Group-size schedule** | fixed G, adaptive G, dynamic sampling, rollout allocation, AERO-style rules | per-step G or adaptive rule |\\n| **L6: Held-out evaluation** | disjoint held-out split, evaluator, sample size, confidence interval, checkpoint-selection rule | held-out protocol separate from training reward |\\n| **L7: Decontamination / parser robustness** | train/test overlap, n-gram or embedding checks, adversarial format-only parser tests, reward parser behavior | probe results and parser tests |\\n\\nThis matches the attached paper’s seven mandatory stack fields, where each field is included because it can flip a comparison: loss form, KL/reference handling, sampler/backend/precision, ZVF/GU telemetry, group-size schedule, held-out evaluation, and decontamination/parser probes. fileciteturn1file8 fileciteturn1file9\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Claim-extraction model\\n\\nLeverTrace uses a **recall-first, schema-constrained claim model**. The model does not simply summarize papers. It converts text into atomic audit records.\\n\\n### Claim tuple\\n\\nEvery extracted claim is represented as:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"C-0042\\\",\\n  \\\"claim_text\\\": \\\"DAPO improves GRPO by using clip-higher and dynamic sampling.\\\",\\n  \\\"source_location\\\": {\\n    \\\"paper\\\": \\\"paper_under_review.pdf\\\",\\n    \\\"page\\\": 6,\\n    \\\"section\\\": \\\"Method\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"claim_type\\\": [\\n    \\\"method_attribution\\\",\\n    \\\"comparison_mechanism\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"subject\\\": \\\"DAPO\\\",\\n  \\\"baseline_or_target\\\": \\\"GRPO\\\",\\n  \\\"metric_or_outcome\\\": null,\\n  \\\"lever_tags\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"lever\\\": \\\"L1.loss_form\\\",\\n      \\\"sublever\\\": \\\"clip_bounds\\\",\\n      \\\"required_slots\\\": [\\\"ratio_formula\\\", \\\"clip_low\\\", \\\"clip_high\\\", \\\"mask\\\", \\\"advantage_norm\\\"]\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"lever\\\": \\\"L5.group_size_schedule\\\",\\n      \\\"sublever\\\": \\\"dynamic_sampling\\\",\\n      \\\"required_slots\\\": [\\\"group_size_rule\\\", \\\"sampling_rule\\\", \\\"compute_accounting\\\"]\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"citations\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"key\\\": \\\"dapo2025\\\",\\n      \\\"anchor_text\\\": \\\"[cite: dapo2025]\\\",\\n      \\\"citation_scope\\\": \\\"same_sentence\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"polarity\\\": \\\"positive\\\",\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": 0.91\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Claim types\\n\\nLeverTrace separates claims into several types because they require different verification standards:\\n\\n| Claim type | Example | What must be checked |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Method-attribution claim** | “GSPO uses a sequence-level IS ratio.” | Does the cited GSPO paper report that ratio and its formula? |\\n| **Implementation claim** | “We use vLLM with bf16 rollouts.” | Does the paper report engine, precision, decoding parameters, tokenizer/template? |\\n| **Comparison claim** | “Method A beats GRPO by 2.4 points.” | Are both stacks reported well enough to compare? |\\n| **Mechanism claim** | “The gain comes from dynamic sampling.” | Is the lever reported and isolated from nuisance stack deltas? |\\n| **Telemetry claim** | “The method avoids collapse.” | Are ZVF/GU or substitute signal metrics reported per step? |\\n| **Capability claim** | “The method improves GSM8K accuracy.” | Is there a held-out split, harness, sample size, CI, and checkpoint-selection rule? |\\n| **Integrity claim** | “The evaluation is uncontaminated.” | Is there a train/test overlap probe and parser robustness test? |\\n\\nThe paper itself gives the reason for treating capability claims specially: online training reward is not capability evidence, and a variant can show a training-reward gain that vanishes on held-out evaluation. fileciteturn1file9\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Extraction pipeline\\n\\nLeverTrace runs in five stages.\\n\\n### Stage A — Document normalization\\n\\nThe bot ingests PDFs, LaTeX, appendices, supplementary files, and linked code/configs. It preserves:\\n\\n```text\\nsection boundaries\\nparagraphs\\nequations\\ntables\\nfigure captions\\nbibliography entries\\ninline citation anchors\\nappendix/hyperparameter tables\\n```\\n\\nTables matter because stack levers are often reported in small experimental-setting tables rather than prose.\\n\\n### Stage B — Candidate claim detection\\n\\nA high-recall detector marks candidate spans using three signals:\\n\\n1. **Citation neighborhoods**: sentences or paragraphs near citations such as “DAPO,” “GSPO,” “Dr.GRPO,” “AERO,” or “GRPO baseline.”\\n2. **Lever lexicon triggers**: words like `clip`, `ratio`, `KL`, `reference`, `vLLM`, `top-p`, `temperature`, `G=`, `group size`, `held-out`, `contamination`, `ZVF`, `reward parser`.\\n3. **Result/attribution syntax**: phrases like “improves over,” “outperforms,” “we attribute,” “uses,” “changes,” “removes,” “normalizes,” “reports,” “does not report.”\\n\\nThe detector is intentionally over-inclusive. False positives are cheaper than missed claims.\\n\\n### Stage C — Atomic claim splitting\\n\\nA sentence like:\\n\\n> “DAPO improves GRPO through clip-higher, dynamic sampling, and better held-out accuracy.”\\n\\nbecomes three separate records:\\n\\n```text\\nC1: DAPO uses clip-higher.                 → L1\\nC2: DAPO uses dynamic sampling.            → L5\\nC3: DAPO improves held-out accuracy.       → L6\\n```\\n\\nThis matters because one citation may support the loss-form claim but not the held-out-evaluation claim.\\n\\n### Stage D — Lever tagging and slot generation\\n\\nA multi-label classifier maps each atomic claim to one or more levers. Then a deterministic slot generator expands the lever into required evidence.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"lever\\\": \\\"L2.reference_policy_kl\\\",\\n  \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The baseline is KL-regularized GRPO.\\\",\\n  \\\"required_slots\\\": [\\n    \\\"frozen_reference_policy\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_location_loss_or_reward\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_coefficient\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_schedule\\\",\\n    \\\"kl_estimator\\\",\\n    \\\"per_token_or_per_sequence\\\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis strictness follows the Min-Report-RL standard: “KL-regularized” is not enough unless the paper reports where KL enters the objective, how it is estimated, and how it is scheduled. fileciteturn1file8\\n\\n### Stage E — Citation resolution\\n\\nThe citation resolver assigns each claim to a target paper or artifact:\\n\\n```text\\nsame-sentence citation     → high confidence\\nsame-paragraph citation    → medium confidence\\nsection-level citation     → low confidence\\nno citation                → MISSING_CITATION\\nTODO placeholder citation  → TARGET_UNRESOLVED\\n```\\n\\nFor the attached paper itself, many references are intentionally TODO stubs, so LeverTrace would not pretend those citations are verified. It would mark claims tied to `dapo2025`, `gspo2025`, `drgrpo2025`, and similar placeholder keys as `TARGET_UNRESOLVED` until real bibliography entries are supplied. The paper explicitly says the bibliography is a manual TODO stub that should not ship as-is. fileciteturn1file12\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Evidence-checking model\\n\\nOnce a claim is extracted, LeverTrace asks a narrower question:\\n\\n> “Does the cited paper actually report the lever detail needed to support this claim?”\\n\\nIt does **not** ask:\\n\\n> “Is the algorithm good?”  \\n> “Is the result real?”  \\n> “Would the gain survive reimplementation?”\\n\\nThat survival question belongs to the controlled audit proposed in the paper. LeverTrace only audits claim-to-report coverage.\\n\\n### Evidence tuple\\n\\nFor each claim, the evidence checker produces:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"E-0042-a\\\",\\n  \\\"target_paper\\\": \\\"dapo2025.pdf\\\",\\n  \\\"target_location\\\": {\\n    \\\"page\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"section\\\": \\\"Algorithm\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"evidence_text\\\": \\\"The objective clips the upper ratio bound at ...\\\",\\n  \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formula\\\",\\n  \\\"matched_slots\\\": [\\\"ratio_formula\\\", \\\"clip_high\\\"],\\n  \\\"missing_slots\\\": [\\\"token_mask\\\", \\\"advantage_norm\\\"],\\n  \\\"coverage\\\": \\\"partial\\\",\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": 0.84\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Verification verdicts\\n\\n| Verdict | Meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| **SUPPORTED_REPORTED** | The cited paper explicitly reports all required lever slots. |\\n| **SUPPORTED_PARTIAL** | The cited paper reports the lever but omits mandatory details. |\\n| **UNREPORTED** | The cited paper does not report the lever. |\\n| **CONTRADICTED** | The cited paper reports a different value or mechanism than the claim states. |\\n| **TARGET_UNRESOLVED** | The citation cannot be resolved, e.g. TODO key, missing bibliography, dead link. |\\n| **OPAQUE** | The cited paper points to a closed backend or artifact whose lever cannot be inspected. |\\n| **AMBIGUOUS_SCOPE** | The claim has multiple citations or a paragraph-level citation and the support cannot be uniquely assigned. |\\n| **NOT_A_LEVER_CLAIM** | The candidate was extracted but does not depend on a stack lever. |\\n\\nFor closed or managed backends, the bot does not infer hidden details. It marks the field `OPAQUE`, consistent with the paper’s point that closed runners should be scoped as a platform implementation rather than treated as fully specified “GRPO.” fileciteturn1file2\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Risk scoring\\n\\nLeverTrace reuses the attached paper’s `grpo-stackdiff` risk vocabulary, but applies it at the claim level.\\n\\n| Risk | Claim-level meaning |\\n|---|---|\\n| **R0 same** | The cited paper reports the lever exactly as claimed. |\\n| **R1 cosmetic** | Only naming/version/cosmetic detail differs. |\\n| **R2 small** | Missing detail is unlikely to affect the claim’s interpretation. |\\n| **R3 material** | Missing or partial lever report could shrink the claim. |\\n| **R4 flip_capable** | Missing/contradicted lever is capable of flipping the comparison. |\\n| **R5 invalidating** | The comparison target is not common or the citation target is wrong. |\\n| **RU unknown** | Evidence is missing, unresolved, or opaque. |\\n\\nThe paper’s own downstream taxonomy includes these risk classes and verdicts for stack comparisons; LeverTrace uses them to prioritize reviewer attention. fileciteturn1file0\\n\\nA missing **L1 loss-form** detail in a claimed 2–5 point GRPO-family improvement is usually high risk, because the paper argues that many GRPO variants are small deltas on the base loop and that unreported stack effects can exceed the claimed method effect. fileciteturn1file6\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Report format\\n\\nLeverTrace emits two reports:\\n\\n1. a **human-readable audit report** for reviewers;\\n2. a **machine-readable JSON report** for CI, reproducibility checklists, and `grpo-stackdiff`.\\n\\n### Human-readable report\\n\\n```markdown\\n# LeverTrace Citation Audit Report\\n\\n## 1. Executive summary\\n\\nSource paper: <paper title>\\nCorpus scanned: <N source papers, M cited targets>\\nClaims extracted: <count>\\nLever-bound claims: <count>\\nFully supported: <count>\\nPartially supported: <count>\\nUnreported: <count>\\nContradicted: <count>\\nUnresolved / opaque: <count>\\n\\nOverall verdict: UNVERIFIABLE | STACK_CLAIMS_PARTIAL | STACK_CLAIMS_REPORTED\\n\\nHighest-risk findings:\\n- C-0042: DAPO clip-higher claim cites dapo2025, but citation target is unresolved. Risk: RU.\\n- C-0051: Baseline KL claim lacks KL coefficient and placement in cited paper. Risk: R4.\\n- C-0068: Held-out improvement claim reports training reward only. Risk: R3/R4.\\n\\n## 2. Coverage by lever\\n\\n| Lever | Claims | Supported | Partial | Unreported | Contradicted | Opaque/Unresolved |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| L1 Loss form | 12 | 5 | 4 | 1 | 0 | 2 |\\n| L2 Reference/KL | 8 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 0 | 1 |\\n| L3 Sampler/backend/precision | 9 | 3 | 2 | 2 | 0 | 2 |\\n| L4 ZVF/GU telemetry | 3 | 0 | 1 | 2 | 0 | 0 |\\n| L5 Group-size schedule | 6 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\\n| L6 Held-out evaluation | 10 | 4 | 3 | 2 | 0 | 1 |\\n| L7 Decontamination/parser | 4 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 0 | 0 |\\n\\n## 3. Claim ledger\\n\\n| ID | Source claim | Lever | Citation target | Required evidence | Evidence found | Verdict | Risk |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| C-0042 | “DAPO uses clip-higher + dynamic sampling.” | L1, L5 | dapo2025 | clip bounds; dynamic sampling rule | citation unresolved | TARGET_UNRESOLVED | RU |\\n| C-0051 | “The baseline is KL-regularized GRPO.” | L2 | baseline paper | frozen ref; KL placement; beta schedule | KL mentioned, beta omitted | SUPPORTED_PARTIAL | R4 |\\n| C-0068 | “The method improves held-out GSM8K.” | L6 | same paper | disjoint split; harness; n; CI | accuracy table only | SUPPORTED_PARTIAL | R3 |\\n| C-0074 | “No contamination affects the benchmark.” | L7 | appendix | overlap probe; parser adversarial test | no probe found | UNREPORTED | R4 |\\n\\n## 4. Per-cited-paper report cards\\n\\n### dapo2025\\nStatus: TARGET_UNRESOLVED\\nClaims depending on this target: C-0042, C-0043, C-0044\\nAction: replace TODO citation with real paper and rerun.\\n\\n### gspo2025\\nStatus: PARTIAL\\nReported levers: L1 sequence-level ratio\\nMissing levers: sampler precision, held-out CI\\n\\n## 5. Reviewer action list\\n\\n1. Require real bibliography entries for unresolved targets.\\n2. Ask authors to report missing L1/L2/L3 fields before accepting any algorithmic-gain claim.\\n3. Treat L6 capability claims as training-dynamics claims unless held-out disjointness, harness, n, CI, and checkpoint rule are reported.\\n4. Queue R4/R5 findings for human review.\\n```\\n\\n### Machine-readable JSON\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"levertrace_version\\\": \\\"0.1.0\\\",\\n  \\\"source_paper\\\": {\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Example GRPO-family paper\\\",\\n    \\\"sha256\\\": \\\"abc123\\\",\\n    \\\"parsed_from\\\": [\\\"pdf\\\", \\\"latex\\\", \\\"appendix\\\"]\\n  },\\n  \\\"summary\\\": {\\n    \\\"claims_total\\\": 87,\\n    \\\"lever_bound_claims\\\": 42,\\n    \\\"supported_reported\\\": 18,\\n    \\\"supported_partial\\\": 11,\\n    \\\"unreported\\\": 6,\\n    \\\"contradicted\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"target_unresolved\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"opaque\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"overall_verdict\\\": \\\"STACK_CLAIMS_PARTIAL\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"claims\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"C-0042\\\",\\n      \\\"claim_text\\\": \\\"DAPO uses clip-higher and dynamic sampling.\\\",\\n      \\\"source_location\\\": {\\n        \\\"page\\\": 6,\\n        \\\"section\\\": \\\"Related Work\\\",\\n        \\\"char_start\\\": 18420,\\n        \\\"char_end\\\": 18473\\n      },\\n      \\\"claim_type\\\": [\\\"method_attribution\\\"],\\n      \\\"subject\\\": \\\"DAPO\\\",\\n      \\\"lever_tags\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"lever\\\": \\\"L1.loss_form\\\",\\n          \\\"sublever\\\": \\\"clip_higher\\\",\\n          \\\"required_slots\\\": [\\\"ratio_formula\\\", \\\"clip_low\\\", \\\"clip_high\\\", \\\"token_mask\\\", \\\"advantage_norm\\\"]\\n        },\\n        {\\n          \\\"lever\\\": \\\"L5.group_size_schedule\\\",\\n          \\\"sublever\\\": \\\"dynamic_sampling\\\",\\n          \\\"required_slots\\\": [\\\"sampling_rule\\\", \\\"per_step_group_size\\\", \\\"compute_accounting\\\"]\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \\\"citations\\\": [\\n        {\\n          \\\"key\\\": \\\"dapo2025\\\",\\n          \\\"resolved\\\": false,\\n          \\\"reason\\\": \\\"bibliography_entry_is_todo_stub\\\"\\n        }\\n      ],\\n      \\\"evidence_checks\\\": [],\\n      \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"TARGET_UNRESOLVED\\\",\\n      \\\"risk\\\": \\\"RU\\\",\\n      \\\"confidence\\\": {\\n        \\\"claim_extraction\\\": 0.94,\\n        \\\"lever_mapping\\\": 0.89,\\n        \\\"citation_resolution\\\": 0.22,\\n        \\\"evidence_verification\\\": null\\n      },\\n      \\\"recommended_action\\\": \\\"Resolve citation target and rerun evidence check.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. How LeverTrace would treat the attached paper\\n\\nFor the attached paper, LeverTrace would extract claims such as:\\n\\n| Claim in attached paper | Lever | Citation key | Likely LeverTrace result |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| “DAPO uses clip-higher + dynamic sampling.” | L1, L5 | `dapo2025` | `TARGET_UNRESOLVED` until real DAPO citation is supplied |\\n| “GSPO changes the importance-sampling granularity.” | L1 | `gspo2025` | `TARGET_UNRESOLVED` until real GSPO citation is supplied |\\n| “Dr.GRPO changes length/normalization in the loss.” | L1 | `drgrpo2025` | `TARGET_UNRESOLVED` until real Dr.GRPO citation is supplied |\\n| “AERO is adaptive-G.” | L5 | `aero2024` | `TARGET_UNRESOLVED` until real AERO citation is supplied |\\n| “ZVF/GU reveals whether the run had usable learning signal.” | L4 | `zvfaudit2026` | `TARGET_UNRESOLVED` because the v1 audit citation is still a TODO |\\n| “The proposed audit holds sampler, KL, LoRA, optimizer, parser, group size, held-out slice, harness, and seeds fixed.” | L1–L7 | same paper | `SUPPORTED_REPORTED` as a proposed protocol, not as completed evidence |\\n\\nThat is not a criticism of the draft; the draft explicitly labels these references as placeholders. It is exactly the kind of issue LeverTrace is designed to surface before submission.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. One-sentence product definition\\n\\n**LeverTrace is a GRPO-family citation auditor that turns prose claims into lever-tagged, citation-linked evidence checks, then reports whether each cited paper actually reports the stack detail needed to make the claim auditable.**\"}]}]"